      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-12-tesla-says-it-will-finally-unveil-the-second-generation-road",
      "title": "Tesla says it will finally unveil the second generation Roadster on October 1",
      "summary": "Tesla’s halo sports car was first announced in November 2017.",
      "body": "12:21 PM PDT · September 12, 2026\nThe long-awaited second generation of the Tesla Roadster might finally be ready for the public.\nIn a post on X on Saturday, the electric vehicle company simply wrote, “Go for launch,” with a graphic showing a “10.01” date — so expect the new Roadster to be unveiled on October 1. The graphic also seems to hint at the promised cold gas thrusters, developed by Tesla CEO Elon Musk’s other big company, SpaceX; those optional thrusters are supposed to allow the Roadster to fly in some way.\nTesla’s second generation sports car was first announced in November 2017 and has been pushed back multiple times. It was even the subject of a social media spat between Musk and OpenAI CEO Sam Altman last year, when Altman said that “7.5 years has felt like a long time to wait” after paying a $50,000 reservation fee, while Musk insisted that Altman had gotten a refund.\nThe Information recently reported that the 2017 version of the car had been completely abandoned for a new, yet-to-be-revealed design that would be revealed imminently.\nMusk has previously acknowledged that even after the big reveal, the Roadster won’t actually go into production for another 12 or 18 months.\nSubscribe for the industry’s biggest tech news",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Anthony Ha",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-12T19:21:19.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/tesla-roadster-2.jpg?resize=1200,822",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/12/tesla-says-it-will-finally-unveil-the-second-generation-roadster-on-october-1/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-12-an-open-letter-to-dario-if-you-mean-it-open-the-weights",
      "title": "An open letter to Dario: if you mean it, open the weights",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://jacob.gold/posts/open-letter-to-dario-amodei-about-open-weights/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49676085\nPoints: 100\n# Comments: 15",
      "body": "You published We Must Pace the Frontier today, which committed Anthropic to embedded third-party evaluators and asked governments to require every other frontier company to match. Sam Altman agreed within hours.1\nI believe you’re being sincere, so I’m asking you to call for a different law, one that would actually slow things down, even though it would require real sacrifice on your part.\nAny AI model a company offers to the public has to be released as open weights.\nThis wouldn’t cover every model you train, just the ones you make available to the public. Internal and research models wouldn’t be affected, and governments could still get access to unreleased models.\nBut Anthropic would have to publish Fable’s weights the day it was released, OpenAI would have to do the same with Astra, and so would everyone else.\nAs you know, funding for frontier model development depends on valuations that assume the weights remain proprietary. By changing that assumption, we can reduce the money available for future training runs and slow progress at every lab at once, without a regulator deciding anything.\nEvery regulation you’ve asked for ends in capture\nThere’s no slowing down frontier models by regulation that wouldn’t lead to regulatory capture. Embedded evaluators, compute thresholds, industry coordination with antitrust waivers. All of it gets written with the help of the current frontier labs, because nobody else understands the technical details well enough to write it.\nOnce it’s law, it only gets more complex, because every incident adds a rule and no rule ever gets removed. The big companies can afford the compliance teams and lawyers to keep up, and each new requirement makes it harder for anyone else to catch them. Instead of slowing you down, it protects your position and profits.\nYou seem like a good and principled person, and you have a record of giving things up for what you believe. You left OpenAI to start a company with safety built in from the beginning, when the safe career move was to stay.2 You’ve spent years asking for regulation of your own industry and getting called a doomer and a regulatory capturer for it.3 You pushed for export controls that shrink your own market. Anthropic paid $1.5 billion to settle with authors rather than fight to the end.4 And today you committed to slowing down before anyone else did.\nYou also had the foresight to make Anthropic a PBC (Public Benefit Corporation), so you can legally put the mission first:\nThe responsible development and maintenance of advanced AI for the long-term benefit of humanity.\nThis is exactly the kind of mission over short-term profit decision the PBC structure exists for. You remain the only person who could propose a law like this and be taken seriously, and the only one likely to actually do it.\nIf you really want to slow down frontier AI, call for a law that makes every publicly available model open weights. There’s nothing in it to game or dodge, even for you.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "routelastresort",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-12T19:15:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://jacob.gold/images/open-letter-to-dario-amodei-about-open-weights.webp",
      "originalUrl": "https://jacob.gold/posts/open-letter-to-dario-amodei-about-open-weights/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
