      "id": "bbc-2026-09-11-as-trump-dominates-convention-some-republicans-quietly-ask-w",
      "title": "As Trump dominates convention, some Republicans quietly ask who comes next",
      "summary": "JD Vance delivered the keynote speech to end the party's convention - but the president still took centre stage, the BBC's Daniel Bush writes.",
      "body": "As Trump dominates convention, some Republicans quietly ask who comes nextFigure caption, Watch: Has Trump won over voters with his midterm convention?ByDaniel BushWashington correspondentReporting fromreporting from DallasRepublicans descended on Dallas this week to turn their midterm prospects around, hoping an unprecedented convention dominated by President Donald Trump would energise voters ahead of the November election.Many left the gathering - billed ahead of time by supporters as a \"Trump-a-palooza\" political fest - just as worried about the party's chances in 2026, and wondering what the Republicans' future will look like after the president leaves office.Vice-President JD Vance, who is widely viewed as a top contender to succeed Trump, offered his a party a glimpse of its potential future during his keynote address on Thursday night.\"We've got to take care of business this November,\" Vance told the crowds. \"Then we'll worry about what comes next.\"His speech was interrupted at one point by chants of \"48\" - a nod to Trump's successor, who will become the 48th US president if they win in 2028. Vance let the chant linger briefly before waving it away.Image source, ReutersOver the two-day event, party activists were split on the wisdom of holding a midterm convention featuring a president who has record low approval ratings, and amid public frustration over the economy and war with Iran.\"I think it will make a difference. If anything it allows us to test the [campaign] message in front of a large audience,\" Jeff Ryer, the Virginia Republican Party chairman, told the BBC.Others said the president's rambling, nearly two-hour convention speech on Wednesday wouldn't sway voters weighing whether to keep Republicans in power in Congress.Polls suggest that Democrats have a good chance to retake control of the House and the Senate is also in play, though viewed as more of a long shot for the party.\"Do I wish Trump gave a shorter speech? Sure, but that's not who he is,\" said one Republican operative, who spoke on the condition of anonymity to offer a blunt view of Trump's convention appearance.The source added that Trump's plea for voters to pretend as if he's on the ballot would fall flat.\"I don't think saying 'pretend I'm on the ballot' will work. He's not,\" the person said.The convention offered a stark reminder that the Trump era is heading toward its final chapter.Trump's presence loomed over the proceedings at the American Airlines Center in downtown Dallas.Republican House and Senate candidates lavished the president with praise in speeches, and supporters showed up wearing the red Maga hats that have become synonymous with Trump's political movement.The gathering was just two days, but still had the look and feel of the traditional four-day conventions staged by both parties in presidential election years. Organisers distributed signs and unleashed a balloon drop at the end as live music blared.Republicans had never held a midterm convention before.Vikki Consiglio, a party activist from Georgia, said it showcased Trump's ability to shake up political norms and capture the nation's attention.Evidence of that came when Trump pledged in his speech to make a payment of $5,000 to all American adults if Republicans retained control of Congress.\"He's a visionary. This whole convention is nothing but a vision,\" Consiglio said.But even as convention goers cheered Trump on, many seemed eager to discuss the 2028 presidential election.\"I'm very concerned about the next 10, 15, 20 years. I'm absolutely thinking about the future of the party,\" said Aiden Buzzetti, the president of The Bull Moose Project, a pro-Trump group.Buzzetti, who is 26, credited Trump with reshaping Republican politics, but said at the same time he was looking forward to a \"new generation of conservative leaders.\"Image source, EPAImage caption, Trump joined Vance on stage to have the final word in DallasIn interviews with the BBC, attendees at the convention most often mentioned Vice-President JD Vance and Secretary of State Marco Rubio as the top contenders to replace Trump.\"Trump needs to pass the torch. We need to look forward,\" said Sandy Money, a Republican from Arizona who attended the convention. \"I love Vance. But Rubio is my choice\" if he runs in 2028, she said.Trump has openly mused about Rubio and Vance's chances to succeed him.But Vance and Rubio have deflected speculation about their political futures.Rubio was on a diplomatic trip to Ecuador during the convention, so he wasn't among the small handful of cabinet officials who attended the festivities in Dallas.The Trump administration has delivered \"a great record of achievement,\" Vance argued, including improving the economy, strengthening border security and lowering the cost of prescription drugs.Vance said the contrast was clear with a Democratic Party that he argued had shifted radically to the left and turned into the \"party of crazy\". The vice-president also made a direct appeal to undecided voters, saying he wanted to win over \"the people who are still making up their minds\".Never one to cede the spotlight, Trump returned to the stage on Thursday night after Vance to round off the convention.For the president, the question of his successor was overshadowed by more immediate concerns.\"You know what happens if you don't vote?\" he asked the gathered crowd. \"You go to hell.\"More on this story",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-11T14:03:43.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/2a2e/live/7023c9c0-addc-11f1-8b98-35ffe039e825.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c2l8xvvw95no?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-11-why-apple-s-next-computer-should-be-for-the-smart-home",
      "title": "Why Apple’s next computer should be for the smart home",
      "summary": "An Apple Home computer could combine a Wi-Fi router, an AI-powered smart home hub, and local storage into a single device. | Photo by Amelia Holowaty Krales / The Verge\t\n\nJohn Ternus didn't mention Apple Home once during his first keynote as Apple CEO. But he did open by describing an \"intelligent personal hub\" that prioritizes local processing, protects your privacy, and integrates with all your ",
      "body": "John Ternus didn’t mention Apple Home once during his first keynote as Apple CEO. But he did open by describing an “intelligent personal hub” that prioritizes local processing, protects your privacy, and integrates with all your other devices, apps, and services. While Ternus was talking about the iPhone — not a new smart home device — what he spelled out is exactly what I want to see Apple make: an intelligent home hub, a computer for your home.\nRumors suggest Apple’s first smart home hub with a screen is imminent. While that’s long overdue, it’s likely to be more interface than infrastructure, a device that can run your home locally but still uses the cloud. What the smart home needs is a computer to run it — one that controls and processes data from connected devices locally, improving speed, reliability, and privacy while securely storing your digital life, from photos and files to music and movies. Crucially, it would do all this inside your home.&nbsp;\nThe cloud has worked well for years, but the recent proliferation of AI-powered home features, such as generating text descriptions from security camera footage, makes local computing more compelling. On-device processing can be faster and more private, and it depends less on an internet connection. These features will also evolve quickly to feed more context to our smart homes, bringing more utility but also a greater need for privacy and security.&nbsp;\n[Image: The category of smart home super hubs emerged at IFA in Berlin this month. This is the Anker Eufy MindBase, a smart home hub that supports dual-band Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, Thread, and Zigbee, and is a Matter controller. It runs an on-device, Anker-developed LLM with support for 16 wireless cameras with expandable local storage up to 48 TB. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/LR6B5731.jpeg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThis type of dedicated home computer — or smart home brain — was an emerging category at last week’s IFA consumer tech show. Anker launched its Eufy MindBase home hub, and Ugreen debuted its HomeAgent, both combining network-attached storage, local AI to process and store footage from cameras, with a smart home hub to connect all your devices.&nbsp;\nBut one piece is still missing: networking.&nbsp;\nMost home routers haven’t caught up with today’s smart home. Despite years of talk about smarter routers, manufacturers and ISPs still seem reluctant to take on the complexity. Comcast has had Thread and Matter support in its gateways since 2022, but hasn’t turned them on. Connecting devices to your home network remains one of the most frustrating parts of the smart home, with manufacturers, ISPs, and platforms all pointing fingers when things fail. Ikea’s recent troubles rolling out new Matter-over-Thread devices are just one example.\nHere’s where Apple could step in and combine all three — a smart home hub, local storage with AI processing capabilities, and a home router — into one device. A hub. AI. A router. Are you getting it? Call it an Apple Home Server.&nbsp;\nThere’s no evidence Apple is building such a thing. But the company’s recent contributions to OpenWrt, the open-source software that powers most routers, could be a tentative first step.\nOne Apple box for your home: Connect it to your modem, and it becomes your Wi-Fi router, Matter Controller, Thread border router, smart home hub, private AI computer, and home server. It stores your files, photos, music, and movies; processes your camera footage locally; runs your automations even when the internet is down; and optionally backs everything up to iCloud. It wouldn’t be another smart home hub; it would be the computer your home needs. Plug it in, sign in with your Apple account, and it just works.&nbsp;\nThat last part is crucial, and plays directly to Apple’s strength: hardware that is “super easy to use without a big learning curve,” as Ternus put it this week. Apple also has experience combining these pieces. AirPort Express put networking and media streaming on one box; AirPort Extreme and Time Capsule added NAS capabilities; and HomeKit Secure Routers brought Apple’s security into the network itself.&nbsp;\nApple walked away from all these products in 2018, saying at the time it might return to the space if it found a meaningful way to progress routers further. The pieces have never fit together more perfectly than they do now.&nbsp;\n[Image: Apple’s Airport Express is a much-missed device. Resurrecting it as part of a powerful whole-home computer for today’s smart home setups would be a popular move. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/airport-extreme-hero.1419969698.webp?quality=90&strip=all]\nApple is deeply involved in developing Matter and is spearheading the Thread networking protocol. An Apple Home Server could bring the entire smart home stack together, letting Apple control everything end to end and remove much of the complexity. Matter even has a blueprint for part of this device: the Matter Home Router Access Point device type combines Wi-Fi networking and Thread Border Router functionality, and Apple’s contributions to OpenWrt focus on HRAP implementation.&nbsp;\nAccording to reporting by Matter Alpha, Apple’s code includes production-grade networking infrastructure that could be turned into a consumer product. There’s no evidence that’s what Apple is doing; it could simply be for router manufacturers. But the pieces are falling into place. With Apple bringing Apple Intelligence to its HomeKit Secure Video service in iOS 27, a Home Server device could take that service to the next level with fully local processing.\nLocal is the key. An Apple Home Server would be a supercharged Apple Home Hub, powering local-first smart home automations, keeping data private and secure at home, and improving responsiveness for devices like smart lights, locks, and security cameras. It could work alongside existing home hubs — the HomePod, Apple TV, and rumored HomePad — for voice and screen interfaces.&nbsp;\nThis might go against the grain. Apple loves subscriptions. But it loves its premium hardware even more, and this would undoubtedly be premium hardware. Also, a device like this would give Apple a powerful foundation for everything it does in the home: iPhones, Macs, iPads, Apple TVs, HomePods, its existing digital services, and potential future AI-powered ones. And, unlike a typical smart home hub, it could appeal to anyone who wants a reliable, private home router and a way to store their digital life at home.&nbsp;\nSmart home infrastructure is broken, and Apple is uniquely positioned to fix it. An Apple Home Server is the hardware backbone Apple Home has always needed. As our digital lives move deeper into the cloud and AI makes the smart home more dependent on data, the case for a computer to run our homes gets stronger, and building one on open standards could limit lock-in.&nbsp;\nApple has all the pieces. The smart home has been waiting for someone to put them together. If Apple finally builds it, the only surprise will be that it took this long.",
