    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-10-bending-spoons-to-buy-collaboration-tools-maker-miro-for-1-3",
      "title": "Bending Spoons to buy collaboration tools maker Miro for $1.36B, 90% less than its 2022 valuation",
      "summary": "Bending Spoons is buying Miro for $1.36 billion, a huge dip in valuation for the workplace collaboration startup, which was valued at $17.5 billion in late 2021.",
      "body": "Bending Spoons is continuing its trend of buying once-sought-after software companies for pennies on the dollar. This time, the Italian company is buying Miro for $1.36 billion in cash (equity value of $1.79 billion), a mighty dip in valuation for the once-hot workplace collaboration startup that was awarded a price tag of $17.5 billion in late 2021.\nFounded in 2011 as a whiteboarding tool called RealtimeBoard, Miro found great fortune during the COVID-19 pandemic, when companies moved to remote work en masse and found their employees wanting to replicate the experience of collaborating on a physical whiteboard.\nMiro quickly capitalized on that momentum by building a platform that could integrate with more than 250 apps and struck partnerships with Atlassian, Cisco, Microsoft, and Zoom. The company also started letting its users build integrations with common tools and customize the base product to meet their needs. Today, it calls itself an “AI innovation workspace” that offers AI assistants for its whiteboard tools, AI workflows, prototyping tools, and AI connectors that pull context from various platforms like GitHub, Jira, and Slack.\nBy 2022, Miro had grown from 5 million to about 30 million users within a scant two years, and its paying customer base had expanded by 550% — factors that likely contributed to its immense valuation at the time.\nBy all indications, the company has continued growing, though not at that blistering pace. Today, Miro has more than 4 million paying users and 100 million total users. Bending Spoons said Miro now has about $600 million in annual recurring revenue, of which 90% comes from businesses and enterprises. The company also has about $435 million in net cash and is profitable.\nStill, the 92% dip in Miro’s valuation illustrates just how much software-as-a-service (SaaS) multiples have unwound since its heyday in 2021. By 2022, the dying pandemic tailwinds spurred companies to tighten spending by cutting down on duplicate apps and licenses. Miro, competing with much-better funded rivals such as Canva, Figma, and Microsoft in the workplace collaboration space, likely found itself shoved aside as businesses started preferring suites of various products instead of individual collaboration tools.\nMiro, which had about 1,200 employees in 2022, cut jobs twice, laying off 119 staff in February 2023 and reportedly another 275 people in October 2024.\nBending Spoons, however, is probably happy it is able to snap up a company that’s been doing quite well for a smidgen of its former, arguably inflated, value. In that way, Miro is pretty similar to Airtable, which was valued at over $11 billion in the boom days of 2021 but sold to Bending Spoons for $1.28 billion last month.\nThe Italian serial acquirer of software companies seems to be exploiting a specific change: large, recognizable SaaS companies that were priced in 2021 as if they’d become software giants, but matured into slower-growing but still substantial businesses with decent recurring revenue and established user bases.\nStill, it’s curious why Miro’s board and investors agreed to sell at that price now, especially seeing that the company didn’t apparently need the cash. Has confidence in SaaS companies being able to go public or find a comparable exit really plummeted that low?\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nRam is a financial and tech reporter and editor. He covered North American and European M&A, equity, regulatory news and debt markets at Reuters and Acuris Global, and has also written about travel, tourism, entertainment and books.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Ram by emailing ram.iyer@techcrunch.com.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Ram Iyer",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T14:34:09.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Miro_Header-2400x1260-copy-1536x806-1.png?resize=1200,630",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/10/bending-spoons-to-buy-collaboration-tools-maker-miro-for-1-36b-90-less-than-its-2022-valuation/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-10-gb-return-after-pregnancy-hard-to-put-into-words-kinsella",
      "title": "GB return after pregnancy 'hard to put into words' - Kinsella",
      "summary": "A year after giving birth, Alice Kinsella takes the latest step on her groundbreaking return to gymnastics at an international meeting for Great Britain.",
      "body": "Olympic gymnast Alice Kinsella says it is \"hard to put into words\" how she feels about returning to international competition as her groundbreaking comeback to the sport continues this weekend.The two-time European champion became the first British artistic gymnast to resume elite competition after giving birth, when she finished fourth in the beam at the British Championships in March.Now the 25-year-old is preparing for her first competition back in Great Britain colours at a senior team international against France, Switzerland, Germany and Italy at Fenton Manor in Stoke on 12-13 September.\"It's kind of crazy - it's such a surreal feeling but I am very excited,\" Kinsella told BBC Radio Stoke.\"It's only been a year, postpartum. Training-wise, it's been tough, but it's been good.  I'm excited to finally get out there. I'm ready, I'm doing everything I can to get back where I was.\"It's been a rollercoaster but a good one.\"Kinsella's pioneering journey back to the sport is so rare it became the subject of a university research paper into elite gymnasts resuming their careers after giving birth.Her son Parker, who was born in September 2025, is already a source of inspiration and he will be watching his mum compete at the weekend as Kinsella competes in all four pieces of apparatus - balance beam, bars, vault and floor - for the first time.\"It's really special because I've wanted to try to get back into a team and this is a really good start because it's a little four-way,\" said Kinsella, who trains with Park Wrekin Gymnastics Club in Shropshire.\"It's not too big of a competition, which is really good. I'm definitely nervous, but it's hard to put into words - it's been so long.\"I did the British [Championships] in March but only did beam and that's all I was training really, but now I'm trying to do all four so it's more on the body.\"It's not like you've done one piece and you can stop thinking about everything, you've got to move on to the next and think about that. So it's been tough.\"After revealing she only took \"three or four weeks off\" after giving birth, Kinsella returned to training at the start of the year with the longer-term intention of being at the World Championships in China in 2027 and the Olympics in Los Angeles in 2028.But, after finding herself ahead of schedule so far this year, she wanted to take the chance to step it up.\"My main goal was to try to get back next year but I'm actually a lot further forward than I thought I would be,\" added Kinsella, who won bronze in the team event at the Tokyo Olympics in 2020.\"So that's why I'm doing this competition because I just want to get out there, compete, because I don't like doing nothing.\"What makes Kinsella's return so unique are the obvious challenges - and changes to the body - pregnancy and childbirth brings.There are many examples of women returning to the top of their sports after becoming mothers but gymnastics, where vital pelvic floor and core strength are both weakened during pregnancy, is different.Kinsella said her gymnastics background has played a big part in her being able to return to physical exercise so quickly but said she \"didn't feel too bad\" when she got back in the gym.\"I always had that goal to get back into the gym and I think when I've got a goal set I will do everything I can to achieve it,\" she said.\"Getting back into a leotard with how my body's changed just feels very weird, but I guess that shows what my body has gone through and I try to think about it in a positive way.\"Her body is not the only thing that has changed with Kinsella adding that parenthood has helped her become better at taking the pressure off herself as an athlete.\"Having a baby changes your life - I'm very patient, whereas before Parker I was not patient at all,\" she said.\"When I made one tiny mistake I would get so annoyed, but now if I just make like a little mistake I'm totally fine about it.\"You have to be patient with kids, they're crazy. So I've definitely changed in that aspect but style-wise and gymnastics-wise like I don't think that's changed.\"Things are harder to get back and to do but I think the more I train it'll probably feel a lot easier.\"Kinsella said when she started her comeback \"no one really had any idea what I was going to able to do\" and wants to keep levels of expectation for her international return as realistic as possible.For her, this weekend in Stoke is there primarily \"just to enjoy it\" and to \"get the experience of all four pieces [of apparatus] again\".\"My body's changed, I can't do the same amount of training I used to do but I'm definitely getting back to that,\" Kinsella said.\"We're definitely on the right track to be where I was before I had Parker. My goal was to start competing next year properly on all four. \"It's going to be really fun. Whatever happens, happens, good, bad. I'm just proud I'm able to be back out there a year postpartum.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T14:33:10.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_sport/1200/cpsprodpb/b96e/live/78e30e90-ad19-11f1-826f-933990eae6f0.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/gymnastics/articles/cly59z2yjpjo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-10-the-iphone-duo-s-hardware-doesn-t-look-special-but-its-softw",
      "title": "The iPhone Duo’s hardware doesn’t look special, but its software might be",
      "summary": "With the iPhone Duo, Apple has pulled off a familiar trick. It arrives into a mature Android foldable market with a handful of hardware features we've mostly already seen elsewhere, but paired with a level of software polish that makes a lot of them feel new again. It's those software flourishes that have me excited to try the iPhone Duo when it launches next month, but I'm less convinced that its",
      "body": "With the iPhone Duo, Apple has pulled off a familiar trick. It arrives into a mature Android foldable market with a handful of hardware features we’ve mostly already seen elsewhere, but paired with a level of software polish that makes a lot of them feel new again. It’s those software flourishes that have me excited to try the iPhone Duo when it launches next month, but I’m less convinced that its hardware will be anything special.\nIf Apple had gotten there first, the Duo’s biggest surprise would have been its squat, wide, passport-inspired shape. But its slow-moving supply chain meant that Huawei, Samsung, and Xiaomi all managed to beat Apple to market with similarly sized rivals, so the unusual design already feels fairly familiar —&nbsp;helped by Samsung’s aggressive efforts to market its new design.\nMore has been made of Apple’s efforts to eliminate the foldable display’s crease, which seem to have been largely successful. Apple attributes it to a 100-component hinge, custom adhesives that allow the display layers to “glide” over one another, and a matte finish that reduces glare and reflections to make the crease harder to spot. Trying the phone at Apple’s event yesterday, Verge editor-in-chief Nilay Patel said that you can’t really see the crease, but that “if you go looking for it, you can definitely feel one.”\nIt sounds, and looks, impressive, but Apple is far from the first to do it. Samsung made great strides in eliminating the crease on its Z Fold 8 and Z Fold 8 Ultra thanks to new titanium display layers. Before that, Oppo all but eliminated its Find N6 crease by using 3D printing to fill the minute gaps across the hinge. I’d have to see all those phones side by side to see which crease is subtlest, but even if Apple does win, it won’t be by much.\nNone of the iPhone Duo’s other standout hardware features are true firsts either. Google beat it to an IP68 rating, now even surpassed by Honor’s IP69. Samsung, Oppo, and Honor have all offered stylus support before — though Samsung has since dropped it. Samsung also moved on from the under-display selfie camera, which has always been hamstrung by poor photo and video quality and a distractingly visible patch on the screen.\nIn other ways, the hardware even feels behind the competition. The Duo is the thickest of this new wave of wide foldables, though at 5.2mm when open it’s still thinner than the iPhone Air. The weight is a more obvious issue: The 254g Duo is 26 percent heavier than the Z Fold 8 — a 53g difference — which is going to be pretty noticeable. You have to go back to 2022’s Z Fold 4 to find a Samsung foldable that’s heavier, TriFold excluded.\nThere’s also no evidence that Apple has adopted silicon-carbon batteries yet, which have allowed Samsung, Motorola, and other Android manufacturers to fit bigger capacities into the smaller spaces of foldable phones. Apple says the phone should still deliver a full 24 hours of regular use, but Honor’s Magic V6 uses silicon-carbon to deliver two days of life.\nThe rear cameras are pretty basic too, with a pair of 48-megapixel shooters and no telephoto. That’s almost an exact match for the cameras on the Z Fold 8, but Google, Motorola, Xiaomi and others are now providing capable triple cameras across their foldable lines, and the iPhone Duo already lags behind.\nNone of this is to say that the Duo looks like a bad piece of hardware. Every foldable phone is a compromise, and Apple has picked a similar set of compromises to Samsung in order to get this phone out the door at $1,999. It looks as good as any Android foldable on paper, but it doesn’t look better than many of them.\nWhere that might change is the software. Google has made an effort to make Android more flexible and adaptable to suit folding phones, while OEMs deliver custom solutions to push things further, but none have rewritten the rulebook quite like Apple.\nThe version of iOS 27 running on the iPhone Duo isn’t the same as on other iPhones, but it doesn’t match the iPad either. The biggest change takes the dock from the bottom of the display and lays it out vertically along the right-hand side. The Dynamic Island is turned vertical and thrown in here too, while the status bar collapses into a small circular icon in the top-right corner. This big change is bolstered by clever little touches throughout, like a dynamic blur effect that shifts as you open the screen, creating a fluidity no Android foldable has so far matched. It’s a total transformation of the iOS layout that specifically has foldables in mind.&nbsp;\nThe point of all that change is to ensure consistency between the outer and inner screens. Open the iPhone Duo on the homescreen and almost everything will stay in place, making the move between screens feel seamless. That effect won’t hold in full-screen apps of course, which will adapt to fill the space, but that right-hand UI rail will be fixed. Locking the whole phone interface to the right-hand edge doesn’t sound great for left-handed users though, who are likely to find the Duo unusually awkward to use unless Apple adds the option to flip everything to the other side.\nStandBy mode has also been retooled for the Duo. Prop the phone down half-open and it will enter StandBy, letting you use it as a digital photo frame, smart display, or just get quick access to a few key widgets, like the clock. Finally, the $2,000 alarm clock you’ve always dreamed of.\nNot everything is as ambitious. Multitasking looks limited so far, with the option to run two apps in split-screen, including two windows of the same app, and save pairs to reopen together. Android rivals offer similar, but support three split-screen apps at once, and often more sitting in flexible, resizeable floating windows.\nBut Apple’s software power doesn’t only lie in its OS. It’s the only phone manufacturer with the influence to shift the app ecosystem at large, and with the Duo it hopes to push third-party developers to adopt its best foldable features. It already showed off Netflix, Slack, and Zoom apps with custom Duo support, in some cases adapting dynamically depending on how flexed the hinge is.&nbsp;\nAndroid foldable features are still rare, with most apps simply changing size to fit the varying screens. Even that has its limits — I found a few apps ran awkwardly on the Fold 8’s short outer display. Apple is likely to have an easier time ensuring developers optimize their apps to suit both of the Duo’s displays.\nMost apps do little else to take advantage of dual displays, multiple cameras, or semi-open states like a tented mode. A few video apps adapt their playback controls when foldables are half-open, but that’s about it so far. Of course, some of those app developers might take their iOS foldable features and eventually roll them out to Android too, so Apple’s advantage might not last forever.&nbsp;\nIt’ll take more time with the Duo to see exactly how it stacks up against the latest models from the Android side of life. So far it doesn’t look like Apple has surpassed its rivals on the hardware side, but its total control of software may give it an edge regardless.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
