    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-10-bbc-drops-drag-race-uk-after-eight-series",
      "title": "BBC drops Drag Race UK after eight series",
      "summary": "Future series will no longer be broadcast on BBC Three as the show moves to a streaming platform owned by its production company.",
      "body": "RuPaul's Drag Race UK is leaving the BBC after eight series, the broadcaster has confirmed.A spokesperson for the broadcaster said it was facing \"very difficult choices\" in the face of \"significant financial pressures\".The show is currently broadcast on BBC Three and iPlayer. Future series - as well as its spin-off vs The World - will be broadcast on Wow Presents Plus, a streaming platform owned by the production company behind the show.At its peak, the UK version of the international drag competition averaged at around 1.5 million viewers per episode.A spokesperson for the BBC said it was \"proud [...] of this groundbreaking show\" and thanked \"RuPaul, World of Wonder and everyone involved, both on and off screen\".Started in 2019, Drag Race UK brought British drag into mainstream entertainment in the UK, and has made household names of drag performers such as La Voix, Danny Beard, and Tia Kofi.The show sees drag queens compete in a series of challenges to impress judges RuPaul, Michelle Visage, Graham Norton and Alan Carr, and often features big name celebrity guest judges.It has become a staple of LGBTQ+ culture and has been praised for its portrayal of the distinct style of British drag.The corporation is currently rolling out plans to make £500m of savings, with director-general Matt Brittin previously telling staff \"the scale of savings requires tough choices\".A BBC spokesperson said: \"As the BBC continues to navigate significant financial pressures and deliver the savings required of us, we have had to make some very difficult choices, none of which have been taken lightly.\"Announcing the news on Instagram, Wow Presents Plus said a \"new era\" of Drag Race UK was on the way.A new series, Drag Race UK All Stars, which sees previous fan favourites return to compete again, will premiere in Autumn 2027 exclusively on Wow Presents Plus. BBC News understands the current series of Drag Race UK, which launched in September 2026, will continue to air as normal on BBC Three and appear on iPlayer, and all existing series will continue to be available once the move is complete.Tia Kofi, the winner of RuPaul's Drag Race UK vs The World series 2 in 2024, stated that the programme has, until now, provided a platform for performers who otherwise lack mainstream representation.The performer told BBC Radio 4's Today programme on Thursday that cutting the show also means a \"marginalised\" audience now has nowhere to go.\"I think the mistake is that they seem to be dropping programmes that they consider to be for more niche audiences, which I just don't think makes a lot of logical sense because the niche audiences are the ones who are very much staying on and watching these things,\" the Drag Queen and singer said.\"But particularly when it comes to programs like Drag Race UK or the cancellation of I Kissed a Boy and I Kissed a Girl, or the Rap Game UK on the BBC as well, the more marginalised audiences have limited representation in mainstream media.\"[They] offer people a platform when they wouldn't have that. There aren't really those alternatives for drag performers in the UK.\"The BBC does represent the LGBQT+ community through storylines in shows including Mr Loverman, Rob & Rylan's Grand Tour, Amandaland, EastEnders, What It Feels Like For a Girl, The Dickie Show, Vigil, Smoggie Queens, Such Brave Girls and Juice.The corporation declined to comment on whether future series of other international versions of the show, including Drag Race Canada and Drag Race Down Under, would continue to air.Earlier this year, the BBC announced it was dropping LGBTQ+ dating show I Kissed a Boy - which made history as the UK's first gay dating show - and I Kissed a Girl.Jordan Burrow, a contestant on series two of I Kissed a Boy, said the decision was an example of \"where we're at within the queer community\".The corporation said at the time that it was \"exceptionally proud\" of the shows but they had to make \"difficult choices in light of our funding challenges\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T07:49:57.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/716f/live/1b5de710-ac86-11f1-ab14-5b9069dc795d.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq63816e60lo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-10-the-first-drink-driving-conviction-may-have-happened-in-lond",
      "title": "The first drink-driving conviction may have happened in London",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.ianvisits.co.uk/articles/the-worlds-first-drink-driving-conviction-may-have-happened-in-london-92107/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49639730\nPoints: 3\n# Comments: 0",
      "body": "On 10th September 1897, a London taxi driver became the first person in Britain — and quite possibly the world — to be convicted of drink-driving.\nAt the time, there were no breathalysers, no legal blood-alcohol limit and the vehicle involved in the historic incident wasn’t even petrol-powered. It was an early type of electric taxi.\nBersey electric cab on display in the Science Museum (c) ianVisits\nThe driver was 25-year-old George Smith, who was driving one of the new Bersey electric cabs. According to news reports at the time, he was driving along New Bond Street around midnight when his cab began swerving across the road, mounted the pavement, and crashed into 165 New Bond Street.\nThe site of the drunken driver’s accident (c) ianVisits\nHe was arrested by Police Constable Russell and taken to nearby Vine Street Police Station, where a police surgeon certified that he was drunk.\nThe following day, he appeared at Marlborough Street Police Court, where he admitted that he had consumed “two or three glasses of beer”. Unsurprisingly, Smith pleaded guilty to being drunk while in charge of his cab and was fined 20 shillings, which would have been the equivalent of about a week’s wages at the time.\nThe magistrate warned Smith that drivers of the new machines needed to be particularly careful. Police, he observed, had developed ways of stopping a runaway horse, but stopping a runaway motor car was another matter entirely.\nA law written before the motor car\nAlthough Smith’s case was a motoring first, Britain already had legislation dealing with drunken people in charge of carriages.\nSection 12 of the Licensing Act 1872 made it an offence to be drunk while in charge of a “carriage, horse, cattle, or steam engine” on a highway or in another public place. The maximum penalty was 40 shillings, or up to a month in prison.\nWhen horseless carriages appeared a couple of decades later, the existing legislation for horsey carriages was used to deal with them as well. But with motoring becoming more popular the previous year, the Locomotives on Highways Act 1896 swept away some of the most restrictive rules introduced for motor cars.\nAnd the speed limit was raised to an eye-watering 14mph, but even so, it would not be until 1904 that driving licences and vehicle registration numbers were introduced.\nSmith was therefore convicted of drink-driving several years before Britain even required him to have a driving licence.\nAnother curious aspect of the story involves one of London’s earliest electric taxis.\nThe Bersey electric cab had entered service just a few weeks earlier. Designed by electrical engineer Walter Bersey, the battery-powered vehicles quickly earned the nickname “Hummingbirds” because of the unusual sound of the electric motors. With a top speed of 9mph, they could travel roughly 30 miles between battery changes. The batteries weren’t recharged in the cab, but swapped at the depot for charged ones, speeding up how quickly they could get back on the road.\nThe Hummingbirds themselves weren’t around for long. Their heavy batteries contributed to tyre wear and mechanical problems, while operating and maintaining the vehicles proved expensive. Just a couple of years after being introduced, the company closed down.\nFrom police judgement to breathalysers\nAt the time, there wasn’t an official test available to determine how much alcohol a motorist had consumed. Police instead relied on how someone behaved, spoke and walked, together with later medical examination and witness evidence.\nEarly devices for measuring alcohol in a driver’s breath were developed in the first half of the 20th century, leading to much more practical breath-testing equipment in the 1950s. It wasn’t until the Road Safety Act 1967 that the first blood-alcohol limit for motorists was introduced, and police were allowed to use roadside breath tests.\nIn the meantime, the corner of New Bond Street and Grafton Street should have a blue plaque marking the ignoble spot where the first drink-driving conviction took place.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "beardyw",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T07:29:21.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://www.ianvisits.co.uk/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/social-cards/92107/facebook.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.ianvisits.co.uk/articles/the-worlds-first-drink-driving-conviction-may-have-happened-in-london-92107/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-10-volvo-xc40-phev-is-back-with-a-new-look-better-sensors-and-g",
      "title": "Volvo XC40 PHEV is back with a new look, better sensors, and Gemini AI",
      "summary": "It's been about three years since Volvo discontinued its plug-in hybrid XC40. The mild hybrid version remains on sale in the US and elsewhere, alongside the electric EX40. Today, Volvo announced it was bringing back the PHEV version and giving it a major facelift. When it arrives at dealerships early next year, the new XC40 will have updated exterior styling, a gut-renovated interior featuring Goo",
      "body": "It’s been about three years since Volvo discontinued its plug-in hybrid XC40. The mild hybrid version remains on sale in the US and elsewhere, alongside the electric EX40. Today, Volvo announced it was bringing back the PHEV version and giving it a major facelift. When it arrives at dealerships early next year, the new XC40 will have updated exterior styling, a gut-renovated interior featuring Google’s Gemini AI assistant, an enlarged infotainment screen, and a new sensor-and-software safety suite.\nAs a hybrid, the XC40 serves as a bridge for customers who want better gas mileage but aren’t ready for the pure EV lifestyle. Volvo itself needs that bridge, after backing down from its pledge to sell only EVs by 2030. That said, the automaker didn’t divulge any details about the powertrain, engine options, electric motor, range, or battery capacity for the XC40 — so we’ll have to wait and see whether this SUV can sufficiently compete in an increasingly cutthroat hybrid market.\nOn the surface, we’ve got a new take on Volvo’s signature “Thor’s Hammer” LED headlights, as well as an updated front grille, bumper, hood and front wings. Same goes for the rear: new lighting, new bumper, and an upgraded tailgate. Additionally, the XC40 is getting a trio of diamond-cut wheel options, available in 18-, 19-, and 20-inch sizes.\nBut the interior is where the meat of Volvo’s improvements comes through. The screen has been bumped up to a higher resolution and a larger, 11.2-inch size. It will run Volvo’s latest user interface, which the automaker designed for faster navigation with fewer taps. Google’s Gemini serves as the voice assistant, using its big AI brain to plan routes, find specific locations and detours, and manage messages. The XC40 rounds it out with an improved wireless phone charger and smart storage solutions.\nWhat about safety? This is a Volvo after all, the automaker most committed to safety (at least according to its well-cultivated reputation). Don’t worry, the XC40 ups the ante in that department with a new software and sensor platform that’s designed to provide real-time collision avoidance, automated emergency steering, and automatic emergency braking.\nThe sensor upgrade includes new front and corner radars, a new front camera, new rear corner radars, 12 new ultrasonic sensors, and new parking cameras. Cloaked in this new sensor suite, the XC40 is getting a host of new safety capabilities, including new door opening alerts that warn occupants before dooring passing cyclists, lane change assist and smoother lane following support on long drives, and an optional 3D/360-degree camera to simplify low-speed maneuvering and parking.\nThe ultimate goal is to build a better XC40 — one that’s safer and more capable, which hopefully translates to better sales numbers and a long, continuous shelf life for Volvo’s tiny SUV.\n“By enhancing the XC40’s design, technology and safety features, we’ve created the best version of the XC40 yet,” says Akhil Krishnan, Interim Lead for the 30 and 40 Series at Volvo Cars, in a statement. “This already popular model is now set to continue being one of our best-selling cars for years to come.”",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Cars",
        "Electric Cars",
        "News",
        "Transportation",
        "Volvo"
      ],
      "author": "Andrew J. Hawkins",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-10T07:00:00.000Z",
