    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-09-an-injustice-or-righting-a-wrong-british-jews-debate-sanctio",
      "title": "'An injustice' or 'righting a wrong': British Jews debate sanctions move",
      "summary": "The UK's decision to sanction trade with illegal settlements has prompted a range of opinion among the British Jewish community.",
      "body": "From his home in the occupied West Bank settlement of Efrat, British-Israeli Rabbi Leo Dee wrote an open letter to Andy Burnham on Wednesday, accusing him of deciding \"to single out Jewish Israelis like me for economic punishment\".The rabbi's British-born wife Lucy, and their daughters Maia and Rina, were killed by Palestinians in the occupied West Bank in 2023. They were shot dead as they drove through the Jordan Valley.\"I am enraged at the injustice of the situation,\" he told the BBC, claiming the government's decision to place sanctions on illegal Israeli settlements, and not the Palestinian authority, was hypocritical.He said Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband was \"living in a different universe\", and argued that the main group who would face hardship as a result were the hundreds of Palestinians who were employed in them.Settlements are illegal under international law, and there has been a rise in violence towards Palestinians in the occupied West Bank by settlers since the start of the Gaza war, which was triggered by the Hamas-led attack on Israel on 7 October 2023.Miliband said the sanctions, announced after Israel opened up tenders for the construction of some 1,200 settlement homes, were necessary to safeguard the possibility of a two-state solution - and to ensure the UK \"not only calls out injustice and suffering, but acts\".Rabbi Dee said he believed that, apart from a small group of \"hooligan settlers\" who could not be controlled and whom most people do not support, many others wanted a different outcome.\"Ninety-nine percent actually want to work closer with Palestinians\" and have peaceful relations with them, he said, just as he did \"before the 7 October attacks\".Another British rabbi was also in the West Bank as the UK government made its announcement - and takes a different view.Rabbi Jonathan Wittenberg was in the Palestinian village of Umm al-Khair, visiting the family of Awdeh Hathaleen, a prominent Palestinian activist and English teacher who was shot and killed by an Israeli settler last year.The Emeritus Rabbi of the New North London synagogue told the BBC he wanted to show that what was happening to Palestinians there mattered to him and his community.Rabbi Wittenberg said he had \"seen with my own eyes several times in the West Bank, the intimidation and threats towards Palestinians - and this needs to be called out\".\"It's terrible for Israel and for Israel's future, it's not what Judaism teaches and it's deeply wrong\", he added.Supported by other Rabbis in the liberal and progressive UK Jewish communities, Rabbi Wittenberg believes that it is possible to support Israel, be concerned for the safety of British Jews, and that it is right for the UK government to take action to protect Israelis and Palestinians.However, he also said he was \"extremely sad that it's come to this\" and that he shared the \"concerns of the Jewish community leadership that people will use this for antisemitism and for demonising Israel even further\".Chief Rabbi Sir Ephraim Mirvis, who represents the Orthodox community, previously  said the sanctions marked a \"truly dark day\" and placed British Jews at greater risk of attacks.In Golders Green - where ambulances run by a Jewish medical charity were set on fire and two visibly Jewish men were stabbed in what police called a terror attack earlier this year - there was concern the government's actions against Israel would increase attacks against British Jews.\"It's so tricky because, in principle, I agree with opposing these new settlements,\" Michael Cohen, who works for a Jewish charity, told the BBC.But he added: \"The timing of it, I think, was completely... just self interest for the Labour Party, signalling before a by-election, before the party conference.\"He said the practical impact of the move would be \"almost zero\".One woman, who runs a jewellery shop that was was twice cordoned off due to incidents in Golders Green, was very critical of the sanctions and said she would \"make it my mission\" to continue to buy Israeli goods whenever she could.\"We have to [support] our own because no one else is going to\", Deborah Miller told the BBC.At the end of the week, Jewish congregations will celebrate Rosh Hashana, the Jewish New Year.Police are already increasing security around Jewish areas to protect communities who have faced multiple attacks this year and are fearful of more.The number of antisemitic incidents recorded across the UK since the start of the year is up by a fifth compared to last year, figures show.The Community Security Trust (CST), an organisation that provides security support to the Jewish community, recorded 1,926 incidents of anti-Jewish hate across the UK in the first six months of 2026.The Crime Survey for England and Wales, external, estimates that only 44% of hate crime incidents between April 2022 and March 2025 came to the attention of the police.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T19:12:52.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/2cf8/live/bc7e8220-ac73-11f1-80f0-9795d1a5edbc.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c9dw048lp9zo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-09-failures-contributed-to-death-of-sir-richard-branson-s-wife",
      "title": "Failures 'contributed to' death of Sir Richard Branson's wife",
      "summary": "Lady Joan Branson died after suffering a cardiac arrest at the private Lister Hospital in Chelsea last year.",
      "body": "A private hospital's failure to provide preventative medication contributed to the death of Lady Joan Branson, an inquest has concluded.The 80-year-old, who was married to Sir Richard Branson, died on 24 November last year after suffering a cardiac arrest at the private Lister Hospital in Chelsea, west London.Senior coroner Professor Fiona Wilcox told Westminster Coroner's Court on Wednesday that Lady Joan did not receive prophylactic anticoagulants at the hospital despite a \"very high risk of developing deep vein thrombosis\".\"I do find her death would have likely been prevented and the lack of anticoagulation did cause or contribute to her death probably,\" she said.The coroner's court heard that Lady Joan was admitted to the hospital on 17 November after a fall weeks earlier on Necker Island, a British Virgin Island owned by her billionaire husband.She died after suffering a pulmonary embolism, a sudden, dangerous blockage in a lung artery, resulting from deep vein thrombosis.Haematologist Dr George Adams told the inquest that Lady Joan was \"extremely high risk\" for blood clots and the \"biggest risk factor by far was her immobility and her fracture\".From her hospital admission to her death, Glasgow-born Lady Joan did not receive any prophylactic anticoagulation, which is used to stop blood clots from forming.The hospital's admitting doctor, Dr Basir Kunduzi, did not complete a bleeding risk assessment on the day of her admission, the inquest heard.Dr Kunduzi said Lady Joan's death led him to \"reflect on my role as a doctor\" and the importance of \"good communication between team members\".Giving her conclusion, Professor Wilcox said it was \"not a case of neglect\" and there \"wasn't an absence of treatment\".After her death, Sir Richard described his wife as the \"shining star around which our family's universe has always orbited\".Listen to the best of BBC Radio London on Sounds and follow BBC London on Facebook, external, X, external and Instagram, external. Send your story ideas to hello.bbclondon@bbc.co.uk, external",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T19:09:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/9be2/live/c5509620-ac75-11f1-80f0-9795d1a5edbc.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5y7k9n7exyo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-09-tate-brothers-to-remain-in-us-jail-as-they-fight-extradition",
      "title": "Tate brothers to remain in US jail as they fight extradition to UK",
      "summary": "A federal judge denied them bail, saying the dual US-UK citizens' wealth and frequent international travel \"constitute quintessential flight risks\".",
      "body": "Andrew and Tristan Tate have lost their bid to be released from a Florida jail as they fight potential extradition to the UK to face charges of rape and trafficking.A federal judge in Miami denied them bail, saying the dual US-UK citizens' wealth and frequent international travel \"constitute quintessential flight risks\".The brothers have been held in US custody since their July arrest, after the UK's Crown Prosecution Service (CPS) unveiled new charges against them. The UK has until 16 September to submit a full extradition request.They will \"continue to defend themselves against the allegations\" and \"engage with the appropriate legal processes in each jurisdiction\", their lawyers said.The two are facing a total of 59 charges in the UK, including rape and counts relating to sex trafficking and indecent images of a child.CPS originally brought 21 charges, but added 38 more in July for alleged actions spanning between 2010 and 2017.US authorities took the men into custody in July after the UK announced the new charges.Last weekend, Romanian prosecutors officially indicted the polarising social media influencers with a range of trafficking and sex offences. The former kickboxers had primarily been based in Romania since 2017, drawing a large audience - primarily young men - through controversial messages about women and images of a luxury lifestyle. Self-proclaimed \"misogynist\" Andrew Tate first rose to fame after a 2016 appearance on the British version of reality TV show Big Brother.The brothers also faced sex-trafficking charges in Romania in 2022, and were under a travel ban until they were allowed to come to the US in 2025.Several prominent US politicians, including Florida's Republican Governor Ron DeSantis, objected to the Tate brothers' arrival in the US.The brothers have denied all charges and allegations.Wednesday's decision means the brothers will remain jailed during what could be a lengthy extradition process.US Magistrate Judge Lauren Louis ruled that, while the Tates \"may not in fact possess what extravagant holdings they portray themselves as possessing online, the record reflects that they possess an exceptional capacity to flee the jurisdiction\".The judge said that their lack of ties to the US and their strong foreign connections \"demonstrate a risk of flight that has not been overcome\".The Tates' lawyers have argued they should be released on bail pending their extradition proceeding, saying the brothers were not a flight risk and did not pose a danger to the community.The lawyers have also argued that they had complied with the terms of their release while facing charges earlier in the UK and Romania.At a hearing on the question of their potential release in August, Andrew Tate said he had \"never committed the crimes I'm accused of.\"Tristan Tate described his detention conditions as small and hot, with little access to sunlight and exercise, adding that he had lost 14 pounds (6.4kg).\"It's beginning to grate on me because I can't partake in helping with my defence,\" he said, according to CBS News, the BBC's US partner. The British Embassy had asked the judge to keep the Tates in custody as the UK sought extradition, writing in court filings that the brothers were \"flight risks\" and claiming they \"would interfere with witnesses if released\".",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T19:09:13.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/25d7/live/35d87ef0-ac83-11f1-994b-c7df927931ed.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cp849rlrrmlo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 3,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-09-apple-watch-s-new-feature-listens-to-your-chats-and-recaps-t",
      "title": "Apple Watch’s new feature listens to your chats and recaps them",
      "summary": "The Siri Recap feature is similar to other note-taking apps like Granola.",
      "body": "At its Surprise and Shine event on Wednesday, Apple launched a meeting notetaker for the Apple Watch Series 12 and Ultra 4, one of a series of new audio intelligence features that can listen to sounds through your Apple Watch.\nThe feature, called Siri Recap, uses ambient listening to take high-level notes of your conversations, according to the company. Apple Intelligence will automatically generate a title, summary, and key points from the conversation, which can then be reviewed in the Siri app.\nApple said that you can choose to keep this feature always on, set up a schedule, or turn off completely.\nThe notetaker wasn’t the only audio-focused feature introduced for the Watch during the event. Apple’s new audio intelligence features also include Shazam, which can identify songs instantly. This means Apple has finally caught up to Google’s Now Playing feature. The Watch can recognize loud sounds like sirens, alarms, doorbells, or a baby crying and notify users.\nApple also introduced a feature called Live Rewind, which lets users recap 15 seconds of conversation when they double-press the Digital Crown.\nApple emphasized that the features don’t store recordings and that raw audio is inaccessible. The Siri Recap feature doesn’t identify speakers either. Users can access Live Rewind recaps, which are end-to-end encrypted. Despite these guardrails, an always-listening feature will likely raise privacy concerns because Siri Recap may still capture sensitive information in its summaries.\nBesides some of the privacy features, Siri Recap sounds similar to other notetakers like Granola and Circleback, which also have Apple Watch apps.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.\nIvan covers global consumer tech developments at TechCrunch. He is based out of India and has previously worked at publications including Huffington Post and The Next Web.\nYou can contact or verify outreach from Ivan by emailing im@ivanmehta.com or via encrypted message at ivan.42 on Signal.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Ivan Mehta",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T19:08:59.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Apple-watch-granola.jpg?resize=1200,675",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/2026/09/09/apple-watchs-new-feature-listens-to-your-chats-and-recaps-them/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-09-hands-on-with-the-new-apple-watch-series-12-and-apple-watch-",
      "title": "Hands-on with the new Apple Watch Series 12 and Apple Watch Ultra 4",
      "summary": "After Apple wrapped up its \"Surprise and shine\" event today, we were able to get a look at the new Apple Watch Series 12 and Ultra 4. \nBoth of the new watches launch September 18th, with preorders available starting today. \nThe Apple Watch Series 12 once again starts at $399 for the aluminum case option in space gray, black, light gold, and dark bronze aluminum. The natural or radiant gold titaniu",
      "body": "After Apple wrapped up its “Surprise and shine” event today, we were able to get a look at the new Apple Watch Series 12 and Ultra 4.\n[Image: Ceramic is back, baby. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/series12ceramic2.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nBoth of the new watches launch September 18th, with preorders available starting today.\nThe Apple Watch Series 12 once again starts at $399 for the aluminum case option in space gray, black, light gold, and dark bronze aluminum. The natural or radiant gold titanium version costs $699, and the night blue or pearl white ceramic costs $899. Apple says this year’s watch offers up to 24 hours of “everyday” battery life, and now offers up to 10 hours of battery during outdoor workouts and supports faster charging with up to 12 hours’ worth recharged in just 15 minutes.\nThe ceramic Series 12 sure does look and feel nice in person. The night blue version is very fetching, but the stark pearl white is also très chic on the wrist. The new dark bronze did look a lot darker in person, however. Like how the dark green Series 7 looked a bit less green and more blackish green, there’s a similar vibe with the bronze.\n[Image: See what I mean about the bronze color? https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/darkbronze.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nThe Series 12 runs on a new S11 chip that powers a few new Audio Intelligence Siri AI features, including Sound Recognition, Live Rewind, and Siri Recap. It also got some health upgrades, including new optical and electrical heart sensors with support for continuous background heart rate sensing that takes readings every five seconds. There’s also a new Health Age feature that compares your health metrics to your chronological age, and a Readiness feature that gives you a score for how to handle the day ahead based on your recent activity, training load, vitals, and sleep score. The Readiness feature will be limited to the Series 12 and Ultra 4, however, because it requires the new heart rate sensor array.\n[Image: You can see the difference between the sensor array on the Ultra 4 and the Ultra 3 (right). https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/sensorultra4.jpg?quality=90&strip=all]\nSpeaking of the array, this is the first major update on this front in a while. You can definitely see the difference side by side. Notably, the Apple reps on hand pointed out that the new Series 12 and Ultra 4 have a “32-petal design,” whereas the older watches had a two-petal design. This refers to the spoke-like lines coming out of the electrode. It should theoretically enable much more accurate readings. You can also see that the optical sensors themselves are situated much closer together. This is to help minimize light leakage.\nThe Apple Watch Ultra 4 also includes the new S11 chip and Audio Intelligence features. It starts at $799 and comes in natural and black titanium, along with a premium Apple Watch Hermès&nbsp;version. Apple says the Ultra 4 offers up to 50 hours of battery life.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Apple",
        "Apple Event 2026",
        "Gadgets",
        "Hands-on",
        "Reviews",
        "Smartwatch",
        "Tech",
        "Wearable"
      ],
      "author": "Victoria Song",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T18:53:48.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/IMG_2485.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/992568/apple-watch-hands-on-series-12-ultra-4",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-09-here-s-how-the-new-iphone-18-pro-stacks-up-to-the-last-gen-m",
      "title": "Here’s how the new iPhone 18 Pro stacks up to the last-gen models",
      "summary": "Apple's release cadence for the latest iPhones is unusual. It'll start with the 18 Pro models, then with the foldable iPhone Duo releasing in October for $1,999. It hasn't confirmed when its base iPhone 18 model will launch. So this post is focused just on the new the 18 Pro and the 18 Pro Max.\nBoth phones are launching on September 18th, with preorders opening Saturday, September 12th. The 6.3-in",
      "body": "Apple’s release cadence for the latest iPhones is unusual. It’ll start with the 18 Pro models, then with the foldable iPhone Duo releasing in October for $1,999. It hasn’t confirmed when its base iPhone 18 model will launch. So this post is focused just on the new the 18 Pro and the 18 Pro Max.\nBoth phones are launching on September 18th, with preorders opening Saturday, September 12th. The 6.3-inch iPhone 18 Pro starts at $1,199, which is $100 more than last year’s phone. The 6.9-inch iPhone 18 Pro Max goes up to $1,299, another $100 jump.\nWith the iPhone 18 Pro being Apple’s 2026 flagship phone, it makes sense to compare it to its two predecessors to see how things have evolved. The biggest differences between the two sizes of this year’s Pro phone are the same as always: the physical dimensions, the battery size, and the size and resolution of the screen. The specs are otherwise identical. But compared to previous Pros, the most meaningful updates come with the cameras, performance, and speed for chewing through AI-related tasks.\nBy holding off on unveiling its base iPhone 18 until a later date, Apple is making deciding on a new phone this fall a little easier — that is, if you’re someone who typically buys the pricey Pro variant. Whether you’re ready to upgrade or not, we’ve got the specs below to make it simple to compare the specs of the most popular high-end phones of 2026.\n[Table: iPhone 18 Pro spec comparison]",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Apple",
        "Apple Event 2026",
        "iPhone",
        "Tech"
      ],
      "author": "Cameron Faulkner",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-09T18:53:43.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Apple-iPhone-18-Pro-color-lineup-260909.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,13.352694054165,100,73.294611891669",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/988501/iphone-18-pro-max-spec-comparison-features",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-09-hands-on-with-the-foldable-iphone-duo",
      "title": "Hands-on with the foldable iPhone Duo",
      "summary": "The first foldable iPhone is finally here, and following its announcement, we got the chance to try the phone for ourselves. We've long been told that Apple would make a foldable iPhone once the tech was good enough - has it been worth the wait?\nIt definitely feels like something different. The inner screen has a decidedly matte finish that handled even some glare from the windows above the hands-",
      "body": "The first foldable iPhone is finally here, and following its announcement, we got the chance to try the phone for ourselves. We’ve long been told that Apple would make a foldable iPhone once the tech was good enough — has it been worth the wait?\nIt definitely feels like something different. The inner screen has a decidedly matte finish that handled even some glare from the windows above the hands-on area well. The folding mechanism is smooth, and even IP68 rated, and the crease is almost impossible to find with my finger. The 5.4-inch outer screen looks petite by modern slab phone standards.\nThe real show happens when you open and close the phone — rather than just switching one screen off and lighting the other one up, there’s a gradual transition that happens, and it looks as if the contents of the screen come into focus as it moves toward its final position. It’s a pretty nice touch.\nI also saw the phone positioned in laptop mode with Netflix on the inner screen, displaying controls on the bottom half before fading them away. There’s also the usual tricks in tent mode, where you’ll get StandBy even if you don’t have the phone plugged in. I’m very curious to see what that does to battery life.\nRumors that a foldable iPhone was in the works have been circulating since at least 2017, with Samsung releasing its first foldable two years after that. Since then, almost every Android manufacturer has entered the market, including Google, Motorola, Huawei, Xiaomi, Oppo, and more.&nbsp;\nOver the last decade, Apple has been variously reported to be developing traditional book-style folding iPhones, smaller flip phone variants, and even larger foldable iPads, before it eventually settled on its final form factor. While early rumors suggested it would be like having two iPhone Airs side by side, the 1.4:1 aspect ratio is squatter, closer to a passport book when closed. That makes the 7.6-inch internal screen wider and better suited for watching videos in landscape than other foldables, but great for reading long scrolling pages or multitasking in portrait.\nThe dual main camera setup shoots 48-megapixel photos and includes a 2x optical zoom. It’s largely the same as the iPhone 18 Pro but lacks the variable aperture.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
