      "id": "bbc-2026-09-08-british-israeli-relations-at-lowest-ebb-in-decades",
      "title": "British-Israeli relations at lowest ebb in decades",
      "summary": "Israel's response to London's trade ban with Israeli settlements in the occupied West Bank shows how important this moment is.",
      "body": "Israel's furious response to the British government's decision to ban trade with Israeli settlements in the occupied West Bank testifies to just how important this moment is.Britain took the brunt of Israel's anger, but the fact that 12 countries chose to move together clearly sharpened the sense of outrage.Officials in London always knew that such a dramatic shift in policy towards Israel-Palestine was going to elicit a hostile response, but seemed taken aback by the speed and ferocity of Israel's reaction.The closure of the UK's consulate in East Jerusalem, which serves the West Bank and deals directly with the Palestinian Authority in Ramallah, and the removal of British representatives from the International Gaza Support Centre, the body set up last year to co-ordinate stabilisation and relief efforts in the Gaza Strip, are very hostile moves.They tell you that Israel is deeply troubled by Tuesday's action by the UK and 11 other countries, which include France, Spain and Canada.Products manufactured in settlements may not represent a significant proportion of Israel's exports, but the political symbolism of singling these out for special treatment is powerful.For decades, the British and other governments have regarded settlements in occupied Palestinian territory as illegal but stopped short of banning products grown or manufactured there.Now, having announced that Britain agrees with the 2024 International Court of Justice ruling that the occupation as a whole - including Israel's military presence - is unlawful, Foreign Secretary Ed Miliband says the government's economic relationships must reflect that.Hence the ban, not just on products but also on companies that provide construction, infrastructure or finance to settlements. The measures, which also include restrictions on arms sales that \"materially contribute to the occupation\", go further than many expected.And it's not just the actions - it's the language of the foreign secretary's heartfelt, sometimes deeply personal statement that is so striking.As a \"proud British Jew... unwavering in my support for the state of Israel,\" Miliband spoke more directly and starkly than practically any of his predecessors.That a British foreign secretary should say that \"settler terrorism is rampant\" and was responsible for the \"ethnic cleansing\" of Palestinians in parts of the West Bank, while the Israeli government \"turned a blind eye to this\" is truly remarkable.Miliband calls this a \"reset\" in British policy. At times today, it felt like a radical rethink, and it leaves British-Israeli relations at their lowest ebb for decades.Yes, the heated, febrile atmosphere that invariably accompanies an impending Israeli election may have contributed to Israeli Foreign Minister Gideon Saar's brutal response, but most Israelis will have shared his sense of outrage.Many Israelis find the violent, messianic ideology of some settlers deeply problematic. Some see them as a national embarrassment. But that's not the same as saying they agree that settlements, built on land captured during the 1967 Six Day War, are illegal.Most would disagree vehemently with the former prime minister, Ehud Olmert, who last year accused his successor Benjamin Netanyahu of turning Israel into an international pariah.But they can feel the walls of international opprobrium closing in - from protests at Eurovision or sporting events, to international arrest warrants being issued against their leaders and now to bans on settlement products - and that makes them anxious and defensive.For the Palestinians, on the other hand, this feels like progress. The Palestinian foreign ministry commended the countries adopting sanctions but called for \"urgent, concrete measures\" to stop Israel's \"illegal settlement enterprise\".In London, the Palestinian ambassador Dr Husam Zomlot called the government's decision \"a turning point in the UK-Palestine relationship\".Britain's \"courageous steps\", he said, would help pave the way for the independence of the State of Palestine on the 1967 borders, with East Jerusalem as its capital.But Zomlot knows that the reality of Israel's 59-year occupation has not ended, or even substantially changed.The prospects for a viable Palestinian state remain vanishingly small, and there's little immediate sign that concerted action by 12 countries will reverse Israel's seemingly inexorable takeover of the West Bank.Only the United States has that kind of influence, should it choose to wield it.In 1991, President George HW Bush and his steely Secretary of State James Baker threatened to withhold $10bn (£7.4bn) in loan guarantees from the government of Prime Minister Yitzhak Shamir, when the famously tough Shamir refused to promise that it wouldn't be used to finance settlement activity in the occupied territories.But Donald Trump has so far shown no desire to curb Benjamin Netanyahu's settlement plans.Some parts of Trump's MAGA movement have turned against Israel in recent years, but the US ambassador to Israel, Mike Huckabee, is a fervent Christian Zionist who shares Israel's view that the West Bank was promised to the Jews by God.So Tuesday's action by the UK and its partners pits them not just against Israel but also against Israel's backer-in-chief in Washington.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-08T21:46:31.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/5143/live/e5381740-ac22-11f1-aed2-8d6da8d75094.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c5ydlex9yn3o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-08-how-to-build-a-printer",
      "title": "How to build a printer",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://nishantjosh.dev/blogs/how-to-build-a-fking-printer/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49617255\nPoints: 410\n# Comments: 91",
      "body": "I was planning to build an e-ink display when I came across the Xteink X3 online. Somewhere in the fine print, it said the thing was fully programmable.\nWhen it arrived, I liked how flat it was. It felt solid in my hand. I started adding things to the CrossPoint firmware: a different boot animation, dice I could roll by shaking the reader, a LinkedIn QR code for SF networking events.\nBut getting stuff onto it was tedious. I had to join its hotspot and open a little upload website in my browser.\nWhile looking for a nicer way to send things to it, a thought struck me. If it looks like paper, it should act like paper.\nI should be able to print on it.\nWhat makes a printer a printer?\nI wanted to open something on my MacBook, press Print, and pick the Xteink. That meant finding out what my computer expected to find at the other end.\nI ended up in the Internet Printing Protocol, or IPP. It lets a computer ask a printer what it supports, submit a document, and ask what happened to the job. The messages travel over HTTP. An operation such as Get-Printer-Attributes asks about capabilities; Print-Job sends the work.\nI advertised monochrome output, 300 dpi, one copy, and one-sided printing. For document formats, I accepted Apple raster and PWG raster. That meant the Mac had to turn the document into pixels before sending it. penguin would shrink the result to fit its screen.\nI declared A5 and Letter paper, media type stationery, and an output bin called face-up.\nI called it penguin. It had the right color scheme.\nTo get driverless discovery on macOS, I also had to add the _universal subtype. That meant calling ESP-IDF’s mDNS API directly, because the Arduino wrapper didn’t expose it.\nGetting the computer to send a page was only part of the job. I still had to receive it on this thing.\nWhere do I put the page?\nA Letter page at 300 dpi is 2,550 × 3,300 pixels. At one byte per grayscale pixel, that’s about 8.4 MB uncompressed.\nThe X3 has 400 KB of RAM, with 16 KB reserved for cache. I needed to run Wi-Fi, run a printer server, and somehow receive an entire fucking page.\nWith Wi-Fi running and the printer’s page image allocated, I had 6.8 KB of heap left.\nI remembered mmap on Linux. Could I do something like that with the SD card and pretend I had more RAM? The C3’s memory-mapping support was for flash, not files on the SD card.\nBut wait. Could I make the display my storage?\nWhat if I passed the incoming page through a transformation pipeline and wrote the result straight to the display? Decode the pixels, shrink them to fit, dither them into black and white. As soon as a row was ready, put it in its place on the display and reuse the working space. Keep going until I have a page.\nThe display already had RAM reserved for its screen image. I could build the page right there as it arrived. Until then, I had been assembling a whole second image just to copy it over.\nMy decoder already worked row by row. I changed the scaler to hand over finished rows too and wired those into the display’s screen image. At first, I let the page appear in bands, like paper feeding out of a printer. Each intermediate refresh took roughly half a second, so I switched to showing the finished page all at once.\nI saved the finished page as a BMP on the SD card using the existing screenshot writer.\nOut of the chip’s 400 KB\nThat gave the network stack room for its socket buffers.\nThere was a penguin in Preview\nI had a sample manga image from Mushoku Tensei on my MacBook for some reason. I opened it in Preview and went to print it.\nThere was penguin in the printer list.\nI selected it and printed. The manga page looked really good on the Xteink. From memory, it took about a second to appear. It’s still there.\nI spent an evening getting to that first print.\nThe printer server runs on the reader itself. I can have it join a Wi-Fi network or start its own hotspot, literate-penguin.\nMy penguin can read now.\nThe code is in my CrossPoint fork, including the printer implementation.\nSaved printouts stay on the SD card, and I can browse them on the reader. My printer has an output tray after all. It’s a folder.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "cat-whisperer",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-08T21:22:11.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://nishantjosh.dev/blogs/how-to-build-a-fking-printer/og.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://nishantjosh.dev/blogs/how-to-build-a-fking-printer/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
