      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-08-100s-of-flights-cancelled-at-uk-airports-due-to-atc-issue",
      "title": "100s of flights cancelled at UK airports due to ATC issue",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/live/c6x2z0yy32ejt\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49614557\nPoints: 70\n# Comments: 32",
      "body": "Live ReportingWatch & listen1SummaryHundreds of flights are cancelled at airports across the UK due to an issue with air traffic controlNats - the air traffic control provider - says the glitch is with its \"flight processing system\" and that a fix has been deployed, later adding recovery was \"taking longer than hoped\"Heathrow, Manchester and East Midlands airports are among those reporting issues - airports in Scotland and Ireland are also experiencing delaysPhil White, travelling to Lanzarote, has been stuck on the runway at Gatwick for hours. \"The pilots have been on every 20 minutes saying they are waiting for information,\" he tells usMeanwhile, Chloe Davis, an aspiring pilot, takes the opportunity to explore the cockpit on her delayed Virgin Atlantic plane to OrlandoRyanair says 65,000 of its passengers have been delayed by up to eight hours while British Airways warns of a \"significant knock-on impact for customers and colleagues\"There's now a backlog of flights and aircraft across Europe in the wrong place - sorting all of this out will take time, writes our correspondentAre you affected?Live ReportingEdited by Caitlin DohertyManchester Airport also expects 'full flight schedule' on Wednesdaypublished at 21:31 BST\nWe have just had a statement from East Midlands Airport, saying it expects a \"full flight schedule\" to operate tomorrow. Manchester Airport, which is part of the same airport operator, Manchester Airports Group, has shared an identical statement at also timestamped to 20:20 BST.It says that though the Nats issue from earlier today is fixed, \"flight disruption is expected to continue for the rest of the day\".\n'Stuck at Heathrow for over three hours'published at 21:28 BST\nPassengers disrupted by delayed and cancelled flights have shared their experiences, watch below: Media caption, 'Stuck on the plane for over three hours': Passengers describe disruption\nDelays and cancellations across the UK as impact of Nats failure continuespublished at 21:15 BST\nImage source, Getty ImagesA quick trawl over the websites of flights across the UK shows a huge amount of disruption continues into the evening. Here's an example of some of the flights that were displaying as cancelled or delayed, shortly after 21:00 BST on Tuesday: At Manchester airport:\n20:35 Ryanair FR3238 to Charleroi\n21:30 Vueling Airlines VY8749 to Barcelona\n16:40 Ryanair FR2162 to Palma has been delayed to an estimated time of 23:30\n20:20 British Airways BA508 to Lisbon\n22:30 AM008 to Mexico City\n20:05 Easyjet EZY411 to London Stansted\n20:40 Ryanair FR5562 to Dublin is delayed to an estimated take-off of 01:50 on Wednesday\nEast Midlands Airport says 'full flight schedule' expected on Wednesdaypublished at 21:11 BST\nEast Midlands Airport says it is expecting to operate a \"full flight schedule\" on Wednesday. In a statement shared on X, and timestamped to 20:20 BST, the airport says that the Nats issue from earlier today is fixed but \"flight disruption is expected to continue for the rest of the day\". The airport adds: \"A full flight schedule is expected to operate tomorrow but the airport may be busier due to rescheduled flights.\"\nLeeds Bradford Airport says four flights have been cancelledpublished at 20:46 BST\nLeeds Bradford Airport (LBA) says it has had to cancel four flights so far due to the national issue with air traffic control.In an update shared with passengers at 19:30 BST, the airport in West Yorkshire said five further flights were left to depart this evening.\"There are ongoing residual delays as the airlines recover service,\"  a spokesperson for LBA said, thanking customers for their patience.\nIn pictures: Queues, screens and delays at Heathrow Airportpublished at 20:31 BST\nWhile the system issue impacting Nats has been resolved, the air traffic control provider admits the recovery of regular flights is taking longer than hoped. That delay has left passengers across the country frowning at screens and flicking through their phones - like these travellers pictured at London Heathrow today.Image source, ReutersImage source, ReutersImage source, Getty ImagesImage source, PA Media\nHundreds of flights cancelled due to technical issue - what we know, what we don'tpublished at 20:21 BSTJack GreyLive reporter\nImage source, ReutersHundreds of flights at airports across the UK have been cancelled due to an air traffic control issue.Here's what we know:\nThe issue has now been fixed, according to air traffic control provider Nats, who said its systems were \"starting to recover\", but later added that the recovery was taking \"longer than hoped\"\nThe BBC understands small numbers of aircraft are being released as “trials” at various airports, with a view to ramping up to more regular flights\nNats said the disruption was caused by an issue with its “flight processing system\"\nThe fault was widespread across the UK, and beyond, with airports in Scotland and Ireland also reporting disruption\nEurope's busiest airline, Ryanair, earlier reported that more than 65,000 passengers had been delayed by up to eight hours\nAnd what we don't know:\nThe exact cause of the fault that led to the disruption, other than it being a technical issue in Nats' systems\nHow long it will take for flights to fully resume and for the backlog to be fully cleared\nWizz Air brands air traffic control provider Nats 'not fit for purpose'published at 20:13 BST\nImage source, Getty ImagesWizz Air has branded air traffic control provider Nats\n\"simply not fit for purpose in its current form\" after a technical\nissue caused hundreds of cancelled flights.In a statement the airline says it is \"extremely\ndisappointed\" that \"another Nats technical failure\" has caused\nwidespread disruption.\"A critical national infrastructure provider should not\nrepeatedly bring the aviation system to a standstill,\" it says.It says \"urgent reform is needed\" and that\nairlines, passengers and airports \"cannot continue to bear the\nconsequences of recurring system failures\".\"We sincerely apologise to affected passengers, but\ntoday’s disruption originates from the UK air traffic control system and is\nentirely outside Wizz Air’s control,\" the statement concludes.Nats declined to comment on Wizz Air's statement when\nRecovery taking 'longer than hoped', says air traffic control providerpublished at 19:50 BST\nAir traffic control provider Nats says recovery from its\nsystem issue that has caused widespread disruption is taking \"longer than\nwe had hoped\".\"Our system issue is now resolved, we are operating\nnormally and working as hard as possible to clear the backlog of flights,\"\nit says in a post on X.\"We are truly sorry for the ongoing flight disruption.\nWe know this is hugely frustrating and apologise unreservedly. Passengers\nshould continue to check with their airline.\"\nStarters served on Heathrow tarmac after stranded passengers unable to disembarkpublished at 19:44 BST\nImage source, Emma ChunEmma Chun is currently tucking into her British Airways\nstarter on the Heathrow tarmac after her flight was cancelled, but passengers\naren’t yet able to get off the plane.She says the flight was due to take off for New York at\n13:20 BST, but all flights were grounded before the plane was given clearance to\ntake off - with passengers since being told the flight had been cancelled.Emma was still on the plane at 18:30. \"We can’t get off the plane as there’s no steps\navailable so they’ve just served our starters,\" she says, adding that\ncabin crew have been able to tell passengers very little.Emma adds that British Airways have instead booked them on\nan alternative flight tomorrow, via Copenhagen, arriving in New York on Thursday\nevening.\"We were due to fly to Buffalo for the day on Thursday to go to Niagra Falls so it means we would also miss that,\" she says.\nAt least 22 flights diverted from Heathrow - here's where they've gonepublished at 19:24 BSTFaye HawkerLive reporter\nSo far this afternoon, at least 22 flights which were due to\nland at Heathrow have been diverted to other UK and European airports. They are: 15:10 Finnair 1335 Helsinki to Heathrow\ndiverted to Stansted15:25 BA268 Los Angeles to Heathrow\ndiverted to Manchester15:35 BA381 Tirana to Heathrow landed at\nGatwick15:45 BA008 Tokyo to Heathrow diverted to\nManchester15:45 SQ 308 Singapore to Heathrow diverted\nto Paris15:45 BA625 Tivat to Heathrow diverted to\nGatwick16:00 AM007 Mexico City to Heathrow\ndiverted to Paris16:00 TK1931 Istanbul to Heathrow diverted\nto Birmingham16:05 BA274 Las Vegas to Heathrow diverted\nto Gatwick16:10 EK031 Dubai to Heathrow diverted to\nBirmingham16:15 VS301 Delhi to Heathrow diverted to\nStansted16:20 NH211 Tokyo to Heathrow diverted to\nStansted16:30 CI081 Taipei to Heathrow diverted to\nFrankfurt16:40 TK1971 Istanbul to Heathrow diverted\nto Brussels16:45 BA717 Istanbul to Heathrow diverted\nto Luton16:55 BA062 Nairobi to Heathrow diverted to\nStansted17:00 BA 689 Santorini to Heathrow diverted\nto Stansted17:35 VS 024 Los Angeles to Heathrow\ndiverted to Manchester17:40 BA705 Larnaca to Heathrow diverted to\nManchester17:45 6E001 Mumbai to Heathrow diverted to\nAmsterdam18:15 BA198 Mumbai diverted to Amsterdam18:40 BA238 Boston to Heathrow diverted to\nSmall numbers of aircraft being released as 'trials', BBC understandspublished at 19:12 BSTBreakingKaty AustinTransport correspondent\nAn airport source says Nats are now releasing small numbers of aircraft as “trials” at various airports, with a view to ramping up to more regular flights.\nMy flight has been cancelled on the way to my own weddingpublished at 19:10 BSTCharlotte SimpsonReporting from Gatwick Airport\nImage caption, Charlotte has her wedding dress, and her fiance, but unfortunately no flightI'm at Gatwick and was due to be on the 17:15 flight to\nBari, Italy, as I'm due to get married In Puglia on Saturday.I’ve got my wedding dress with me, I’ve got my fiance with\nme, but sadly we are not going to be going anywhere tonight.Our flight is among those that have been cancelled this\nafternoon because of this disruption with air traffic control.It's pretty chaotic at the moment, people have just been\nwondering around looking for help - but from what we've seen they've just had\nto wait around to be told over the public address system - like us - that their\nflight is cancelled.Everyone has been rushing on the phone, trying to call\nvarious airlines to try and get re-booked.Luckily our wedding isn't until Saturday, so we do have a bit\nof time. But it isn't the tranquil start to our wedding celebrations\nthat we had imagined unfortunately.\nFrustration among passengers in Malta as flights to UK cancelledpublished at 18:57 BSTManish PandeyReporting from Malta International Airport\nI’m at Malta International Airport, where my flight home to\nLondon Gatwick has been cancelled.The departures board initially showed “delayed” to all\nUK-bound flights when we arrived, before changing the 21:30 BA flight to\ncancelled. The official at the British Airways counter has told us all\nflights to the UK have been cancelled because of the air traffic issues.There are a significant number of travellers heading home to\nthe UK. We’ve been told that accommodation is being worked on, and\nwe will be told through the BA app what the next steps are.There is frustration from lots of passengers, and the coffee\nshop in the airport is filling up quickly with exhausted travellers.For now, the wait continues.\nBritish Airways says fault will have 'significant knock-on impact' for customerspublished at 18:45 BST\nImage source, PA MediaBritish Airways says it has been informed by the air traffic\ncontroller that the \"fault has been resolved\" but due to the severity\nof the issue, there will be a \"significant knock-on impact for customers\nand colleagues\".A spokesperson for the airline says it was forced to cancel\nor divert more than 100 flights this afternoon, \"inconveniencing tens of\nthousands of customers\".This incident was \"entirely out of our control and\nincredibly frustrating for our customers and colleagues,\" it says, with\naircraft and crews ending up out of place.\"We ask customers whose flights have been cancelled not\nto travel to the airport and to bear with us if they’re trying to get in touch,\nas our call centres are incredibly busy at the moment.\"Customers due to travel today or tomorrow are able to\nmake changes to their booking on ba.com,\" it adds.\nPhones being frantically checked and queues for empty check-in desks at Gatwickpublished at 18:39 BSTTheo WhyteReporting from Gatwick Airport\nThere is a long queue snaking back from the service desk at Gatwick airport. People are frantically checking departure screens and their\nphones for updates. Others are calling airlines in an attempt to find alternate\narrangements. At the airline check-in desks, passengers are queuing up in\nfront of empty Iberia desks, hoping someone will appear and give them some information.\nGovernment must improve resilience of air traffic control - shadow transport ministerpublished at 18:24 BST\nShadow transport minister has criticised the government\nand Nats for allowing another air traffic control incident after a major outage brought\nUK airports to a standstill last year.“Yet again passengers are stuck on the tarmac wondering why\nBritain cannot keep its own skies open,\" Greg Smith says.He adds that Transport Secretary Heidi Alexander\n\"promised after last year’s Swanwick fault that she would work with Nats\nto prevent this happening again. It has happened again.“The Government and Nats must explain what has happened and\nhow they will make sure resilience is improved.”Earlier,\nAlexander said she was \"aware\" of the technical issue and\nHeathrow Airport runs out of space due to flights not departingpublished at 18:03 BSTKaty AustinTransport correspondent\nWhat’s really noticeable here at Heathrow Airport is that the skies have fallen quiet. My understanding is Heathrow reached the point this afternoon where it couldn’t take in any more arriving flights because things hadn’t been departing - and basically the airport ran out of space.\nLong queues of frustrated passengers at Dublin Airportpublished at 17:49 BSTAlfie TobuttBBC Your Voice\nImage caption, Long queues are forming at Dublin AirportThe queue for Ryanair’s ticket desk here at Dublin Airport stretches almost the entire length of the Terminal 1 departures hall.There are so many frustrated passengers in the queue that they are blocking the doors at the entrance to the terminal.While the air traffic control group Nats says it has implemented a fix and systems are “starting to recover”, it will be a while before the situation returns to normal.\nHow Scottish airports are responding to the disruptionpublished at 17:37 BSTKaty ScottBBC Scotland news\nFlights at Scottish airports have also been disrupted by the technical issue at Nats air traffic control.Glasgow and Aberdeen say some services may experience delays and it is advising passengers to check with their airlines before travelling.Edinburgh says Nats has implemented a fix, but knock-on disruption is likely throughout the day as operations recover.Airports in Inverness and the Highlands and Islands say London flights are experiencing “significant delays”, but passengers should check-in at their normal time and check for updates.Glasgow International Airport has seen 18 flights cancelled today, while 29 have been cancelled at Edinburgh Airport.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
