      "id": "bbc-2026-09-08-lindsay-clancy-s-lawyer-hopes-for-deal-with-prosecutors-afte",
      "title": "Lindsay Clancy's lawyer hopes for deal with prosecutors after mistrial",
      "summary": "During an appearance on Good Morning America, Kevin Reddington also asked Trump to pardon Clancy.",
      "body": "The lawyer for the Massachusetts woman accused of murdering her three children says he hopes to reach an agreement with prosecutors, after the case ended in a mistrial on Friday. Kevin Reddington told ABC's Good Morning America programme that he was upset a lone member of the jury would not acquit Lindsay Clancy. To find Clancy guilty, jurors had to be unanimously convinced beyond a reasonable doubt that she intentionally killed her children - Cora, Dawson and Callan - and was not experiencing a psychotic break.On ABC, Reddington also appealed to President Donald Trump to pardon Clancy. Because she was charged by the state, though, only the Massachusetts governor has the power to pardon her.\"I would hope that you [Trump] would consider this young lady, the type of person she is, what she's been through, and consider a pardon,\" Reddington said.Reddington also said he hoped the district attorney prosecuting the case \"would be able to work something out that would be acceptable to both sides\".The BBC has contacted the district attorney for Plymouth County, Massachusetts.\"I'm willing to listen to Tim,\" Reddington said about District Attorney Tim Cruz, calling him a \"hard-charging prosecutor\".The jury - nine women and three men - was weighing a range of options, from not guilty by reason of insanity to manslaughter and first-degree murder. The 12-member panel appeared to nearly reach a consensus, except for a single holdout who, Reddington said, \"would not apply the law of reasonable doubt\" as instructed by the judge.\"That's not right,\" he said. \"And that's why I was upset.\" Throughout the trial, prosecutors called dozens of witnesses, including several doctors, seeking to prove Clancy had made a calculated decision to murder her children in the basement of their Massachusetts home in January 2023.Clancy does not deny killing the three, but says there was no premeditation and she was experiencing psychosis at the time. For her to be convicted of first-degree murder, a jury would have to believe she planned the killings.The BBC has contacted the governor's office about a possible pardon request.Under Massachusetts law, Reddington could also formally ask the judge to rule independently that Clancy was not guilty based on evidence presented during the trial. He has five days to file that motion. Currently, the case rests with the prosecution, who must decide whether to seek another trial.With additional reporting from Ione Wells",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-08T17:30:39.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/e811/live/4fb37d80-aba6-11f1-8e72-a346833ed029.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/czrz6gxp033o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-08-ai-power-users-claim-anthropic-duped-them-with-subscriptions",
      "title": "AI power users claim Anthropic duped them with subscriptions, and they’re taking it to court",
      "summary": "Anthropic says power users are key to its business - it's prioritized them even when it means cutting off other popular applications, like OpenClaw. But some of these same customers say Anthropic misled them into believing they'd get more out of a top-tier pricing subscription than they did. \nIn an expanded class action lawsuit filed today, a group of Claude subscribers say the company deceptively",
      "body": "Anthropic says power users are key to its business — it’s prioritized them even when it means cutting off other popular applications, like OpenClaw. But some of these same customers say Anthropic misled them into believing they’d get more out of a top-tier pricing subscription than they did.&nbsp;\nIn an expanded class action lawsuit filed today, a group of Claude subscribers say the company deceptively advertised the limits of its Max subscription tier. The lawsuit was brought by attorneys Monica Vaca and Kati Daffan, who both formerly worked at the Federal Trade Commission under Lina Khan. It’s a rare attempt to legally penalize AI companies for a widely held point of frustration: the industry’s growing money squeeze.&nbsp;\nAnthropic’s Max plan for Claude is an upgrade to the $20/month Pro plan. It has two pricing options: $100 per month for “5x” the usage limits of Pro or $200 per month for “20x”. But the lawsuit alleges that these numbers, advertised in Anthropic’s marketing graphics, are misleadingly couched in fine print. Anthropic actually says you’ll get 20x or 5x the usage that the Pro plan offers, but it’s only promised for five-hour chunks of time that are subject to a weekly limit — which the complaint alleges adds up to a much smaller total increase in usage capacity.\nTo understand this, “you’ve got to dive deep,” Vaca said. It requires clicking two different hyperlinks to see the word “session,” and then to see what “session” meant, you’d need to separately go to the Pro plan webpage for an expanded definition.&nbsp;\nComplaints about Max’s terms have cropped up online. One Reddit user wrote, “The weekly allowance is what the pricing page makes you think you’re buying. The rolling 5-hour window is what actually controls whether you can work … It’s like giving someone a bigger gas tank while keeping the fuel pump limited to one gallon every five hours.”&nbsp;\nOver the past eight months or more, as pressures to turn a profit increase, AI customers have frequently complained that top labs are passing on their steep operating costs. Although Anthropic announced the Max plan in April 2025, it didn’t impose the allegedly deceptive weekly limits until a few months later in August, as the company pushed to compete with OpenAI. In Anthropic’s latest model release, Fable 5.1, the company hinted at broader cost concerns among customers, writing that it was “addressing the feedback we’ve received from customers on price” in the new model’s pricing.&nbsp;\nThe complaint was filed in July before being withdrawn and refiled as an expanded class action. In a motion to dismiss the earlier case, Anthropic argued that it didn’t necessarily omit the session limits from its marketing; rather, it says it’s technically available to the consumer if they know where to look. “Accessing this clarifying information … required nothing more than clicking hyperlinks available in the purchase process—the digital equivalent of flipping a product over to read the back label,” Anthropic wrote in the motion to dismiss.&nbsp;\nAnthropic did not respond to a request for comment.\nVaca disagrees that it’s that easy to understand the terms. “This is hard for consumers —&nbsp;they don’t know what’s in that black box,” she said. “They have to rely on the claims the marketer is giving. There’s no way for them to audit it. There’s no way for them to know exactly what it is they’re going to get… They’re basically taking a leap, and they’re believing in an honest marketplace.”&nbsp;\nShe added that another reason the case was “compelling” for her is that she and Daffan started hearing from people who felt they needed to pay high-priced subscription costs for AI services in order to avoid becoming irrelevant in the job market. But oftentimes, she said, they felt they weren’t getting what they paid for, which was frustrating for a service that costs between $100 and $200 per month.&nbsp;\n“[Daffan] and I worked at the Federal Trade Commission for 38 years combined,” Vaca said, adding, “There is a long line of precedent on false advertising. It’s commercial speech. You can’t lie when you’re marketing a product.”&nbsp;\nEven if Anthropic has the stipulations somewhere on its website, Vaca said, it shouldn’t be up to the consumer to have to seek that out.&nbsp;\n“It’s a ‘buyer beware’ approach,” she said. “And that’s really not fair to people.”",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "AI",
        "Anthropic",
        "Exclusive",
        "Law",
        "Policy",
        "Report"
      ],
      "author": "Hayden Field",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-08T17:27:31.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/03/STK202_DARIO_AMODEI_CVIRGINIA_D.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.732984293194,100,78.534031413613",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/ai-artificial-intelligence/990313/anthropic-class-action-lawsuit-pricing-subscription-plans",
      "readTimeMinutes": 4,
      "language": "en"
