      "id": "bbc-2026-09-08-prince-george-begins-first-day-at-a-rainy-eton-college",
      "title": "Prince George begins first day at a rainy Eton College",
      "summary": "The 13-year-old will follow in the footsteps of his father, the Prince of Wales, by attending the elite private school.",
      "body": "Prince George arrived on a rainy morning for his first day at Eton College, accompanied by his father and mother, the Prince and Princess of Wales.Prince William and Catherine carried umbrellas as they brought George, wearing a jacket and tie, to be welcomed by the head teacher, Simon Henderson, and provost Sir Nicholas Coleridge.\"Welcome, good luck and enjoy,\" said the head teacher, shaking hands with Prince George.They made small talk about the bad weather before the new pupil, parents and staff disappeared inside the historic school, where 13-year-old George will be a boarder.George, who had travelled the short journey from his home at Forest Lodge in Windsor, was seen with his parents arriving at Eton's school yard, overlooked by the school's Tudor landmark of Lupton's Tower.He was later photographed wearing the school's uniform, with black tailcoat, waistcoat and a stiff white collar.The future king follows in the footsteps of his father in attending the elite private school, which has taught 20 British prime ministers and where fees are around £65,000 a year.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-08T11:52:40.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/aa0f/live/a466d6d0-ab81-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cwyzel3qjvzo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-08-monty-don-to-leave-gardeners-world-after-more-than-20-years",
      "title": "Monty Don to leave Gardeners' World after more than 20 years",
      "summary": "The presenter said it had been \"deeply rewarding\" but added that \"now is the right time for a change\".",
      "body": "Monty Don is to step down as the host of Gardeners' World after more than 20 years, the BBC has announced. The 71-year-old, who is the programme's longest-serving head gardener, will leave at the end of the current series.In a statement, Don said working on the show had been a \"deeply rewarding experience,\" but added: \"Now is the right time for a change.\"The BBC said Don had \"inspired and guided millions of viewers with his warmth, wisdom and passion for gardening\" since joining the programme in 2003.Don began presenting the programme at Berryfields, a rented garden in Stratford-upon-Avon, before it moved in 2011 to his own garden, Longmeadow in Herefordshire. \"Throughout that time - and around 700 programmes - I have had the privilege of sharing my own abiding passion for gardens and gardening with an equally passionate, and often equally knowledgeable audience,\" he said.\"That connection with millions, not just in Britain but across the world, who share a love of gardens, plants and the natural world is something I have always treasured and which I increasingly believe to be at the heart of a life well lived.\"Don added he had \"always relished the craft of programme making\" and paid tribute to the \"superlative BBC team\" he worked with over the years. \"I look forward to continuing to work with and for the BBC on other projects, as well as following new avenues,\" he concluded. \"And of course, I will still be gardening, dog by side, here at Longmeadow.\"The presenter, whose full name is George Montague Don, made his TV debut in 1989 with a gardening segment on ITV's This Morning.That led to further work on the BBC and Channel 4, a newspaper column, and the publication of more than 20 books about gardening authored by Don.He replaced Alan Titchmarsh on Gardeners' World in 2003, but took a break from the programme in 2008 as he recovered from a stroke.He has also presented other programmes including Around the World in 80 Gardens, My Dream Farm, Mastercrafts, Monty Don's Rhineland Gardens, and coverage of the Chelsea Flower Show.The BBC's chief content officer Kate Phillips described Don as \"unique\", adding he was \"a wonderfully engaging broadcaster with a soothing, calm presence who has inspired so many\".\"Monty's decision to leave Gardeners' World is one I respect, but I'm still very sad he's leaving,\" she added.\"We are a nation full of budding gardeners, and his tips and advice have been invaluable to viewers for more than 20 years and have certainly helped me increase my knowledge as well as my plant survival rate!\"I would like to thank Monty personally for his honesty and kindness which has seen him open up about his own personal struggles and the power of the natural world to improve mental health. We will miss him on the show hugely.\"Don has been open about his struggle with depression over the years.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-08T11:43:32.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/36c9/live/5d567720-ab7f-11f1-a291-b542ee92de7c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c74ew4402kxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-08-investigation-after-female-staff-at-eiffel-tower-replaced-by",
      "title": "Investigation after female staff at Eiffel Tower replaced by men for religious visit",
      "summary": "The move saw outraged staff walk off the job on Monday, closing the French landmark for a day.",
      "body": "An investigation has been launched in France after female staff at the Eiffel Tower were replaced by male colleagues for the visit of a Hindu religious group.A delegation of around 100 people linked to the Bochasanwasi Akshar Purushottam Swaminarayan Sanstha (BAPS) group visited the iconic landmark in Paris on Saturday.\"Women were asked to leave their places of work\" and felt \"humiliated\", staff union representative Diane Davoine said. The tower closed on Monday as staff went on strike to show their outrage.BAPS - a movement whose beliefs originate in the 18th Century - strictly separates men and women, including in temple activities and building entrances.Ariel Weil, who is the president of the company that runs the Eiffel Tower, blamed its managers for being \"a bit overzealous\" in complying with the request without considering whether it was \"inappropriate\". Weil, who is also mayor of Paris Centre, told French broadcaster Franceinfo on Tuesday that the incident was \"totally shocking and unacceptable\".An internal investigation will determine who was responsible for putting the conditions in place, Weil said, and ensure it \"never happens again\".In a statement reported by AFP news agency, staff at the monument said that during Saturday's visit, female employees were also \"instructed not to go through or cross certain areas while the delegation was present\".\"Tensions mounted over the weekend, so [on Monday morning], the employees decided to meet: first, to open a dialogue with management, and second, to ensure that women are never treated this way at the company again,\" Davoine said.Société d'Exploitation de la tour Eiffel (SETE), which runs the Eiffel Tower, had previously said the conditions imposed on female staff were not in line with its values \"nor with the principles of equality between men and women\".BAPS consecrated its first major temple in France on Sunday, in a Parisian suburb.In a statement on X, BAPS said the trip was \"arranged in conjunction with the Eiffel Tower team at the start of normal opening hours to avoid disrupting or inconveniencing others\".It apologised \"for any pain or inconvenience caused\", adding \"we believe in the universal values of mutual respect and service\".On its website, the group describes itself as a \"spiritual, volunteer-driven fellowship dedicated to improving society\" through Hindu values.It has temples in major cities around the world, including London, Los Angeles and Sydney.It draws its beliefs from the teachings of Lord Swaminarayan, a late 18th-Century celibate ascetic who is regarded by his followers as an incarnation of Vishnu, the revered Hindu god of preservation.BAPS says the segregation of men and women was introduced for the benefit of women, but critics say 18th-Century beliefs have no place in today's world, and keeping women out in the name of religious practices is discriminatory.Politicians in France have condemned Saturday's incident.Yaël Braun-Pivet, the president of France's National Assembly, said: \"I refuse to allow women to be compelled to step aside in order to meet the demands of foreign visitors.\"French hard-right leader Marine Le Pen said: \"In France, we will never accept that the presence of women be 'limited'.\"Former centre-right French Prime Minister Gabriel Attal, who has said he will run for the presidency next year, said: \"In France, no culture, no belief, no worship, nothing nor anyone can come to dictate to women their place.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
