    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-07-ecoflow-makes-the-miniature-power-station-even-smaller",
      "title": "EcoFlow makes the miniature power station even smaller",
      "summary": "The River 260 Gen4 (on the table) powering a portable fridge, Starlink Mini, and charging a phone. | Image: EcoFlow\t\n\nIf you're in the market for a tiny power station that punches well above its size and weight then have a look at EcoFlow's new fourth-generation River series. \nThe River 260 Gen4 features a 256Wh capacity battery while the 520 Gen4 packs in 512Wh - storing about 2.5x and 5x the ene",
      "body": "If you’re in the market for a tiny power station that punches well above its size and weight then have a look at EcoFlow’s new fourth-generation River series.&nbsp;\nThe River 260 Gen4 features a 256Wh capacity battery while the 520 Gen4 packs in 512Wh — storing about 2.5x and 5x the energy of the largest airline-approved power banks, respectively. Better yet, the batteries are built using longer-lasting LFP chemistry that’s far less volatile.&nbsp;The River 260 Gen4 features four USB ports, and 300W of max continuous output delivered out its lone AC jack. The River 500 Gen4 features the same USB port selection but offers a pair of AC jacks good for 500W of continuous output.&nbsp;\nBoth of EcoFlow’s Gen4 power stations feature class-leading energy density that cuts both the weight and size, making these power stations lighter and more compact than similarly-specced competition. That makes them ideal for an overnight camping trip or photo shoot in the literal field. The EcoFlow 520 Gen4 is powerful enough to keep a standard US fridge running for about 4 hours during a blackout.\nHere’s a quick comparison to show just how much energy these remarkably small power stations are packing, starting with the River 520 Gen4:\nAnd here’s how the River 260 Gen4 compares to some of the main competition:\nBoth models can serve as a UPS for your computer, with a cutover time of less than 10ms, and feature one state-of-the-art 140W USB-C PD3.1 port that’s bidirectional. That little trick lets these power stations eschew charging bricks in favor of direct DC charging from a 140W USB-C wall charger you might already own, or a portable USB-C solar panel EcoFlow will happily sell you.\nEcoFlow has also addressed a common problem with all power stations that causes them to eventually die if left idling too long. By minimizing the energy spent converting stored DC to AC output, more watt-hours remain to charge your drone, bike computer, and phone, or to power your Starlink Mini and compact fridge.\nThe River 260 Gen4 will be available in Europe from Q4 2026, while the River 520 Gen4 will follow sometimes in Q1 2027. Pricing for both will be announced as we get closer to their global release dates.",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Energy",
        "IFA 2026",
        "News",
        "Science",
        "Tech",
        "Work anywhere"
      ],
      "author": "Thomas Ricker",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-07T14:59:41.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/IFA_R4_0.3kWh_Outdoor.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,3.0977312390925,100,93.804537521815",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/science/991033/ecoflow-makes-the-miniature-power-station-even-smaller",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
