    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-07-new-inbetweeners-film-confirmed-by-netflix-as-lead-stars-ret",
      "title": "New Inbetweeners film confirmed by Netflix as lead stars return",
      "summary": "Netflix has confirmed the original stars of the coming-of-age sitcom are set to reprise their roles for the film.",
      "body": "Ooh, sequel friend! A new Inbetweeners movie is officially in the works, Netflix has confirmed. The original cast of Simon Bird, James Buckley, Blake Harrison and Joe Thomas will reprise their roles for the feature film, with production starting on Monday.The Inbetweeners 3 will follow on the story of their characters, Will McKenzie, Jay Cartwright, Neil Sutherland and Simon Cooper.They originally starred in three seasons of the popular coming-of-age sitcom, which aired on E4 between 2008 and 2010.It followed their trials and misadventures as suburban teenagers at the fictional Rudge Park Comprehensive as they navigated school life and friendship.The show has gained new fans over the years due to some of its best bits finding new life on social media, as well as through the film sequels.The third instalment follows two sequels backed by Film4, which were released in 2011 and 2014. Alongside the announcement, Netflix released a teaser video which showed Will, Simon, Neil and Jay return to their iconic yellow Fiat. The friend group all get into the tiny car but Simon struggles to start the engine. After some encouragement, it gets going and they drive out of what looks like a storage unit. Production kicks off after months of speculation that there would be another film, but beyond the lead characters returning, the rest of the cast is yet to be confirmed. Fans will be wondering if English actress Emily Atack will return in her role as Charlotte Hinchcliffe, or if Emily Head will come back as Carli D'Amato.However, Netflix has announced The Inbetweeners creators, Damon Beesley and Iain Morris, will return to write and direct the film.Beesley and Morris said it has been \"super fun\" hanging out with friends again. \"We're going to work hard every week to ensure there are no problemos making this new film for humans,\" they added. The first film sees the group of friends head to Crete in Greece for a post sixth form break, but they quickly find more trouble than they bargained for. The 2014 follow-up reunites Will, Simon, and Neil as they travel to Australia to visit Jay during his gap year - where they find out his travels aren't as lavish as his messages had claimed.Tensions rise as they clash over whether to chase cultural experiences or a wilder time on their trip.  The first film made history as the biggest opening weekend for a UK comedy at the time, according to Netflix, while the sequel also broke box office records.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-07T11:03:33.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/de56/live/c2d84b40-aa95-11f1-ab09-7f2415c9a652.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cx2z0je34y1o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-07-vmware-migration-reduces-tottenham-hotspur-s-licensing-fees-",
      "title": "VMware migration reduces Tottenham Hotspur's licensing fees by 85 percent",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://arstechnica.com/information-technology/2026/09/vmware-migration-reduces-tottenham-hotspurs-licensing-fees-by-85-percent/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49596797\nPoints: 71\n# Comments: 35",
      "body": "Tottenham Hotspur, a professional soccer team that’s part of the Premier League, has saved over 85 percent in licensing fees by replacing its stadium’s VMware instance with Hewlett-Packard Enterprise’s (HPE’s) Morpheus VM Essentials (VME) virtualization software.\nTottenham hasn’t disclosed which VMware products it used or how much it previously paid the Broadcom firm.\nThe soccer organization confirmed this week to The Register that it has moved its stadium’s server, storage, and networking infrastructure to HPE solutions delivered through HPE’s hybrid cloud management platform, GreenLake. That is all “underpinned by” VME and HPE’s OpsRamp software for hybrid and multi-cloud environments, Rob Pickering, Tottenham’s CTO, told the publication, with HPE in charge of the hybrid cloud-managed service.\nTottenham is almost done with a broader redesign of its stadium’s data center and network infrastructure, The Register reported. The stadium has a data center with six aisles that uses HPE’s ProLiant Compute Gen12 server hardware and Alletra Storage MP. In an announcement this week, HPE noted that Tottenham is using “solutions including HPE Morpheus software and HPE OpsRamp software… to manage a complex, multi-site environment more efficiently while modernizing virtualization platforms and reducing operational overhead.”\n“We’re at the right time of our replacement cycles to be looking at those decision points,” Pickering told SDxCentral this week.\n“For at least as long as my career in technology, hypervisor has been one of those very unsexy things that you bought from one person and you just sort of got on with it. And you know, with VMware, a 400-pound gorilla in that space, they took some decisions that I think run counter to both the system integrator side and the customer side.”",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "joozio",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-07T11:01:06.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/Tottenham-1152x648.jpg",
