      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-06-i-m-teaching-an-introductory-12-week-course-on-quantum-oracl",
      "title": "I'm teaching an introductory 12 week course on Quantum Oracle Engineering",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://shukla.io/quantum-oracle-engineering/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49586151\nPoints: 39\n# Comments: 15",
      "body": "Most quantum speedup claims depend on an oracle that exists only on\npaper. This course teaches the craft of building practical quantum\nChoose the problem, build the oracle\nCPU, GPU, QPU: three devices, three workloads\nthe QPU’s job: fewer samples for an average\nthree questions: task randomness, precision, oracle\nGrover on a database loses to data loading\n2. The Monte Carlo speedup\na query count is not a runtime\nthe payoff qubit’s angle encodes the win\namplitude estimation reads that angle to precision\nGo fails question 1, the bandit\nSway: gaps of 10⁻⁴ on a 32×32 board\nthe same oracle shape fits an epidemic model\nthe contract: board, two moves, randomness tape, payoff\none round: Black places, White places, every stone\nthe register layout in Qiskit\na uniform move choice over the legal cells\nthe d20 as a 5-bit comparison against a neighbor\n3×3, two rounds: 169 qubits\namplitude estimation runs the rollout forward and\ndecide from the old board, write to a shadow board,\nin-place updates read a neighbor that already\nerase move-selection scratch before the board\none payoff qubit, everything else inverted\nthe qubit and gate count as the board grows\nreversible circuits have no delete\nBennett: compute, copy out, uncompute\nthe inverse must see the same inputs as the forward\npeak scratch sets the qubit count\nclean scratch is necessary, not sufficient\nthe textbook says never measure mid-circuit\ncompilers measure scratch to reclaim qubits\nGidney’s AND†: an X-basis measurement instead of a\na random sign, fixed by one phase gate\nhalf the T gates of an adder\nsafe when scratch holds a basis function of the\nthree scratch classes: clean, borrowed, conditionally\nQiskit passes the reuse condition as unchecked\na block can destroy its own condition\ntwo correct blocks, one unguaranteed boundary\nrestoration types: Hoare contracts over\na 12-bit oracle: 20 qubits to 13\ntruth tables cannot see a phase\ncertificates replayed by a Lean kernel\ngate-by-gate checking needs closure under the gate\npast Toffoli, assertions grow exponentially\none theorem per family, checked in\nCount it, test it, judge it\n9. Where the quantum lives\na process that runs step by step\nbetween any two steps, classical bits would do\nno one classical carrier works for all steps at\nthe SHIFTS channel: one qubit in, two out, built to\nthe quantum lives in the memory between steps\nrunning n copies does not amortize\nquantum memory: zero or linear in n, nothing\nthe same law for preparing states\nSHIFTS: at least 0.03 qubits per copy\na test with single-qubit measurements only\na correct device passes every time\nq qubits of memory pass with probability at most\ntoo little memory fails exponentially fast\nthe device stays a black box\n12. Audit the next claim\nthe AI era’s assumption: compute closes every\nwall: tiny gaps, irreducible randomness\nweak baselines, query counts sold as runtimes\nignored parallelism, solver randomness as task\noracle cost hidden behind “assume oracle\nthe three questions on a headline claim, live",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "BinRoo",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-06T13:00:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://shukla.io/quantum-oracle-engineering/img/myth01.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://shukla.io/quantum-oracle-engineering/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
