      "id": "theverge-2026-09-05-cd-sales-are-booming-as-physical-media-continues-its-resurge",
      "title": "CD sales are booming as physical media continues its resurgence",
      "summary": "There’s some real gems in there. | Photo: Terrence O’Brien / The Verge\t\n\nAccording to the Recording Industry Association of America (RIAA), CD sales exploded in the first half of 2026. A new report from the organization says 17.5 million CDs were sold in the first six months of the year, up from just 12 million during the same period in 2025. That's a jump of 45.7 percent, and 58.6 percent in doll",
      "body": "According to the Recording Industry Association of America (RIAA), CD sales exploded in the first half of 2026. A new report from the organization says 17.5 million CDs were sold in the first six months of the year, up from just 12 million during the same period in 2025. That’s a jump of 45.7 percent, and 58.6 percent in dollars, which reached $171.1 million. What makes this all the more shocking is that CD sales actually shrank 22 percent from 2024 to 2025.\nThe RIAA isn’t the only one to notice the boom in CD sales. In July, research firm Luminate released a report saying that 16.3 million units were sold in the first half of 2026. It suggested that much of that increase was due to special editions from K-Pop acts like BTS and that Gen Z and millennials were treating them mostly as collectibles, saying that only about half of buyers actually owned a CD player.\nVinyl continued its resurgence as well, with the number of units sold climbing by nearly 21 percent from last year to 26.5 million. “Other physical” media, which includes CD singles, DVDs, cassettes, SACD, DVD-Audio… basically anything besides “standard vinyl and CD albums,” also surged by over 73 percent, to 1.6 million units.\nDespite the increased interest in physical media and the ongoing iPod revival, people are not leaving streaming in droves. Streaming revenue grew by 4.7 percent to $4.9 billion — dwarfing all physical media combined.\n[Image: Those are some big jumps in sales. https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/Screenshot-2026-09-05-at-12.22.57-PM.png?quality=90&strip=all]",
      "source": "The Verge",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [
        "Entertainment",
        "Music",
        "News"
      ],
      "author": "Terrence O’Brien",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-05T16:49:33.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://platform.theverge.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/2/2026/09/DSCF1155-EDIT.jpg?quality=90&strip=all&crop=0,10.737667300434,100,78.524665399133",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.theverge.com/entertainment/990794/cd-sales-are-booming-as-physical-media-continues-its-resurgence",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-05-how-the-tobacco-industry-drove-the-rise-of-ultra-processed-f",
      "title": "How the Tobacco Industry Drove the Rise of Ultra-Processed Foods (2025)",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://vcresearch.berkeley.edu/news/how-tobacco-industry-drove-rise-ultra-processed-foods\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49577985\nPoints: 118\n# Comments: 75",
      "body": "In 1963, a U.S. tobacco company bought Hawaiian Punch. It marked the beginning of the tobacco industry's entry into the food sector and led to an explosion of hyperpalatable, chemically-engineered foods — and a dramatic rise in obesity.Follow Berkeley Talks, a Berkeley News podcast that features lectures and conversations at UC Berkeley. See all Berkeley Talks.In the early 1960s, R.J. Reynolds, one of the largest and most profitable tobacco companies in the U.S. at the time, wanted to diversify its business. Its marketing strategies had been highly successful in selling its top brands, like Camel, Winston and Salem cigarettes, and executives thought, Why not apply the same strategies to, say, the food industry?So in 1963, R.J. Reynolds acquired Hawaiian Punch. It marked the beginning of the tobacco industry’s entry into the food sector. In the following decades, R.J. Reynolds and Philip Morris expanded aggressively into the food industry, acquiring major brands, like Del Monte, Nabisco, General Foods, Kraft and 7UP, where they produced hyperpalatable, chemically-engineered foods now known as ultra-processed foods, or UPFs. These products were marketed especially to children and other vulnerable groups. In Berkeley Talks episode 229, Laura Schmidt, a professor of health policy in the School of Medicine at UC San Francisco, discusses how ultra-processed foods — like cookies, sodas, instant noodles, fish sticks and cereals — are a direct legacy of the tobacco industry, and are responsible for a dramatic rise in obesity, diabetes and other chronic diseases across the country. “About 60% of the calories in Americans’ diets are from ultra-processed foods,” says Schmidt, who spoke at a UC Berkeley event in May. “In the mid-’80s, when we see ultra-processed foods starting to scale up in the American food supply, we also see obesity starting to really rise. That is the moment when some of the largest food companies are owned by tobacco companies.”This talk took place on May 5, 2025, and was co-sponsored by the Berkeley Food Institute (BFI) and Berkeley Public Health. It was moderated by Isabel Madzorera, an assistant professor in food, nutrition and population health at Berkeley Public Health and co-faculty director at the Berkeley Food Institute.Watch a video of the event on the Berkeley Food Institute’s YouTube page.Read the transcript.Listen to other episodes of Berkeley Talks",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "paimapi",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-05T16:14:34.000Z",
      "imageUrl": null,
      "originalUrl": "https://vcresearch.berkeley.edu/news/how-tobacco-industry-drove-rise-ultra-processed-foods",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "bbc-2026-09-05-the-centuries-old-problem-of-mapping-the-world",
      "title": "The centuries-old problem of mapping the world",
      "summary": "As the UN adopts a new world map to reflect Africa's true size, let's take a closer look at what different maps get right and wrong.",
      "body": "The now widely used Mercator map was created in 1569 by Flemish cartographer Gerardus Mercator.Its main purpose was to help European sailors navigate the oceans, as cartography Professor James Cheshire at University College London explained.\"He wanted a straight line to be drawn on a map to look like a straight line in reality if you followed a compass bearing,\" he told the BBC. \"That was what it was intended to do, and that's what it does brilliantly well.\"To keep those angles correct on a flat map, Mercator had to stretch the grid lines wider as they moved away from the equator toward the poles.His map accommodates for the curvature of the Earth by making countries near the equator seem much smaller than they are, with countries nearer the poles appearing stretched.The distortion is a result of it being impossible to accurately represent the surface of a globe on a two-dimensional chart.More than 450 years on, Mercator's creation is still the basis for many of the maps we see in classrooms, on TV and online, including Google Maps.But critics of the Mercator map say there is a psychological effect to viewing some places as smaller than they actually are and others as larger, as we instinctively view bigger things as more important.For Kenyan geographer Kioko Muendo, this has implicitly downplayed Africa's vast resources, population and investment opportunities.\"Maps are more than just guides for travellers - they shape how regions are perceived politically and economically,\" he told the BBC.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-05T16:14:24.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/e80e/live/d224a510-a92f-11f1-bb81-8fbd2d2d397c.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cly5r60v4mro?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-05-wikimedia-foundation-workers-overwhelmingly-vote-to-form-uni",
      "title": "Wikimedia Foundation Workers Overwhelmingly Vote to Form Union with CWA",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://wikiworkersunited.org/announcements/2026-09-04-us-wikimedia-foundation-workers-overwhelmingly-vote-to-form-union-with-cwa/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49577975\nPoints: 176\n# Comments: 66",
      "body": "San Francisco, Calif. – U.S.-based workers at Wikimedia Foundation—the nonprofit organization that hosts Wikipedia\nand sister projects—have won their union election, voting overwhelmingly to join Communications Workers of America (CWA)\nLocal 9415. The victory marks an important milestone in a global movement among Wikimedia Foundation workers to\nestablish collective bargaining through the legal frameworks available in their respective countries.\nU.S.-based workers began organizing as a part of the broader Wiki Workers United global union campaign of Wikimedia\nFoundation workers committed to strengthening worker voice, transparency, equity, and shared stewardship.\n“The recognition of our union’s first government-sanctioned bargaining unit is a huge milestone in our over ten-year\njourney to educate, agitate, and organize coworkers,” said Bryan Davis, a WWU organizing committee and WWU-US/CWA\nmember. “We continue our petition for recognition in the U.K. and hope to see more jurisdiction-specific units call for\nvoluntary recognition in the coming weeks and months. Solidarity will continue to drive our discussions, planning, and\nactions as we begin working on our collective goals for contract bargaining to support all WMF workers regardless of\nlegal jurisdiction, job duty, or hours of employment.”\nIn July, U.S.-based workers demanded union recognition after a supermajority of them signed union authorization cards.\nWhen management refused to uphold the organization’s commitment to respect the rights of workers to freely choose union\nrepresentation, workers persevered through a union election overseen by the National Labor Relations Board (NLRB).\n“Today’s victory is a demonstration of the power of international solidarity,” said Decovan Rhem, President of CWA\nLocal 9415. “WWU-US/CWA members organized across their workplaces and across the world, building support among\ncoworkers internationally and among an incredible community of volunteers and wiki editors. Now, we will take the power\nour members have built to the bargaining table to negotiate a strong contract that gives Wikimedia workers the voice\nWWU extends its sincere gratitude to the Wikimedia volunteer community. Over 2,000 volunteers and community members who\nhave collectively made more than 15 million edits have\npublicly signed petitions supporting Wikimedia Foundation workers\nand affirming the importance of giving workers a meaningful voice in shaping the future of the movement. This petition\nis the most supported petition in history on the English-language Wikipedia. The WWU Organizing Committee is deeply\nappreciative of this extraordinary demonstration of solidarity.\n“Today’s election result is a well-deserved victory not only for Wikimedia U.S. staff, but for everyone who believes in\na free, democratic internet. I am proud to be among the 2,000 volunteer editors celebrating this achievement today,”\nsaid Shushugah, a volunteer Wikipedia editor since 2015 with over 14,000 global edits.\nThe Campaign to Organize Digital Employees (CODE-CWA) is a union movement of thousands of\nworkers fighting every single day to build the voice and power necessary to ensure the future of the tech, games, and\ndigital industries in the United States and Canada. CODE-CWA is a project of the Communications Workers of America\n(CWA), which represents hundreds of thousands of workers throughout tech, media, telecom, and other industries who stand\ntogether to fight for justice on the job and in our communities.\nMedia inquiries: info@wikiworkersunited.org, Sean Nesmith snesmith@cwa-union.org\n-- WWU-US Branch September 4, 2026",
