      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-04-what-will-apple-s-john-ternus-era-look-like",
      "title": "What will Apple’s John Ternus era look like?",
      "summary": "It’s officially the Ternus era at Apple.   Tim Cook stepped down as CEO this week, handing the company to former hardware chief John Ternus, whose first memo promised a “huge launch next week” — timing that puts Apple’s next iPhone event on his desk before he’s even settled in. Cook isn’t going far, though: he’s staying on as Executive Chairman, focused on the kind of policy […]",
      "body": "It’s officially the Ternus era at Apple.\nTim Cook stepped down as CEO this week, handing the company to former hardware chief John Ternus, whose first memo promised a “huge launch next week” — timing that puts Apple’s next iPhone event on his desk before he’s even settled in. Cook isn’t going far, though: he’s staying on as Executive Chairman, focused on the kind of policy relationships that recently turned something as small as a map label into a very public balancing act. All of which raises an obvious question: what does the Ternus era look like, and how much rope will shareholders give him to figure it out?  On this episode of TechCrunch’s Equity podcast, hosts Kirsten Korosec and Sean O’Kane unpack what Ternus is walking into, why he may actually be better positioned to make progress on software than hardware in this new AI era, and more of the week’s news.\nSubscribe to Equity on YouTube, Apple Podcasts, Overcast, Spotify and all the casts. You also can follow Equity on X and Threads, at @EquityPod.\nTheresa Loconsolo is an audio producer at TechCrunch focusing on Equity, the network’s flagship podcast. Before joining TechCrunch in 2022, she was one of 2 producers at a four-station conglomerate where she wrote, recorded, voiced and edited content, and engineered live performances and interviews from guests like lovelytheband. Theresa is based in New Jersey and holds a bachelors degree in Communication from Monmouth University. You can contact or verify outreach from Theresa by emailing theresa.loconsolo@techcrunch.com.",
      "source": "TechCrunch",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "Theresa Loconsolo",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-04T17:18:00.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/GettyImages-2287301688.jpg?resize=1200,800",
      "originalUrl": "https://techcrunch.com/video/what-will-apples-john-ternus-era-look-like/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 1,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "theverge-2026-09-04-agi-is-whatever-you-want-it-to-be",
      "title": "AGI is whatever you want it to be",
      "summary": "OpenAI announced its next big model, GPT-6 Astra, and also, by the way, that \"the AGI era\" is here now.\nToday on The Vergecast, we've got an all-star panel to break down the news of the week.\nFirst, senior AI reporter Hayden Field joins us to talk about our supposed AGI era and Nvidia's acquisition of Hugging Face. \nThen, our friend Joanna Stern pops in to talk about new Apple CEO John Ternus, old",
      "body": "OpenAI announced its next big model, GPT-6 Astra, and also, by the way, that “the AGI era” is here now.\nToday on The Vergecast, we’ve got an all-star panel to break down the news of the week.\nFirst, senior AI reporter Hayden Field joins us to talk about our supposed AGI era and Nvidia’s acquisition of Hugging Face.\nThen, our friend Joanna Stern pops in to talk about new Apple CEO John Ternus, old Apple CEO Tim Cook, and most importantly, to speculate on the format of next week’s Apple keynote.\nWatch | Listen | Get ad-free\nWe wrap things up with a super-special lightning round from the IFA electronics show in Berlin. Senior reviewer Jennifer Pattison Tuohy and editor Dominic Preston each pick out the five most interesting gadgets they saw at the show.\nAnd two more things: Vergecast producer Travis Larchuk pops in to tell his tale of identity theft woes and warn you that he isn’t the one DMing you on Twitter to ask you to be on The Vergecast. Plus, Brendan Carr is a dummy.\nIn case you missed it this week: David Imel talked to the CEO of Fairphone and had a lovely conversation with Victoria Song and Elizabeth Lopatto about what it means to be human. Plus, Lauren Feiner joined us to talk through her reporting on the data center capital of the world, and Jennifer Pattison Tuohy fired up the Vergecast Rank-o-Matic to run through the top Apple Home rumors ahead of next week’s keynote.\nWe want to hear all your thoughts about all of it! Call the Vergecast Hotline at 866-VERGE11, send us an email at vergecast@theverge.com, and tell us everything that’s on your mind. And make sure you subscribe so you don’t miss an episode!",
