      "id": "bbc-2026-09-04-schools-close-early-for-a66-crash-funeral",
      "title": "Schools close early for A66 crash funeral",
      "summary": "The funeral of Jakub Matusiak, one of seven killed in the A66 collision, is held near Middlesbrough.",
      "body": "A number of schools have closed early amid safety concerns over a funeral linked to a crash which killed seven people.  The funeral of Jakub Matusiak, 23, one of five young men killed in the head-on collision with a marked police car on the A66, has been held near Middlesbrough.The crash happened near South Bank on 22 August when a Volkswagen Passat travelled the wrong way on the dual carriageway and hit a police car.  Two police officers, PC Matthew Blades, 37, and PC Tom Clough, 38, were also killed.Head teachers has warned parents large crowds were expected to gather in the South Bank area for the funeral, with traffic disruption likely.Cleveland Police said it was expecting the event to cause traffic disruption and delays, adding the force had plans in place to ensure public safety.The decision to close the schools was taken following discussions between local head teachers and Redcar and Cleveland Council and was not a police recommendation, it said.In an open letter, Conservative MP Laura Trott urged Education Secretary Lucy Powell to ensure local schools remained open and called for sufficient police support to be provided to keep schools in the area safe.Speaking to BBC Radio 4's Today programme, Ms Powell said she trusted head teachers to \"make judgments that they need to make to keep their school community safe\". She said: \"As Education Secretary, I want schools to be open. I want pupils attending schools all the time.\"These head teachers of these small number of schools have taken a very difficult decision, with the local authority and the police, for the safety of their school community to close early.\"What I would say, is that there are huge anxieties and worries in the communities.\"She said she had been in touch with local MPs and Home Office ministers to put extra policing and resources in place to \"calm those tensions down and to make those streets safe\".She added: \"This is a very, very difficult and delicate situation, as you say, and I will always put the safety and wellbeing of children at the forefront of anything I do.\"Supt John Wrintmore from Cleveland Police said there was potential for traffic disruption and delays from 15:00 to 17:00 BST on Friday.\"This will affect areas of South Bank, Normanby Road through Teesville, Ormesby and Marton, and Acklam Road,\" he said.\"We are aware of some school closures in the area and have liaised with partners in the local authorities regarding the expected disruption today. \"Any decisions made by schools or other services to close has been a decision made by those organisations in isolation, and has not been a police recommendation.\"We are working closely with highways departments from the local authorities, who are leading on the traffic management for this event, in order to minimise disruption.\"There will be a proportionate visible police presence in the affected areas to ensure public safety, in line with our plans for any significant gathering.\"A spokesperson for Redcar & Cleveland Borough Council said: \"A number of schools in the South Bank area will close earlier than usual on Friday afternoon due to anticipated traffic congestion and transport disruption associated with a local funeral taking place in the area.\"The decision was made after careful consideration of the potential impact on home-to-school transport and the departure of pupils, staff and families at the end of the school day.\"Schools have worked with the Local Authority and transport providers to consider arrangements for pupils, including those who travel to and from specialist educational settings.\"We recognise that early closure may cause inconvenience for some parents and carers, and we thank them for their understanding and cooperation.\"The decision comes after a vigil last week, for one of the young men who died, was followed by disorder in Haswell, County Durham, where a number of cars were set on fire.The incident prompted criticism from MPs and police.The Volkswagen Passat involved in the collision was being pursued by police until 10 seconds before the crash, according to the police watchdog.The Independent Office for Police Conduct said the Passat was believed to be using cloned registration plates.Police said a total of 24 people had been arrested as part of a wider police operation against organised crime, following the crash.Follow BBC Tees on X,, external Facebook, external, Nextdoor and Instagram, external.",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "world",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-04T15:16:20.000Z",
