      "id": "bbc-2026-09-04-firmus-energy-customers-face-price-increase",
      "title": "Firmus Energy customers face price increase",
      "summary": "Customers in the Ten Towns area will see a 8.98% increase and 12.5% for Greater Belfast.",
      "body": "Firmus Energy has announced an 8.98% price increase for customers in the Ten Towns area and 12.5% for Greater Belfast.The Ten Towns new tariff will be effective from 1 October and equates to an extra £7 per month on the average bill for customers.In greater Belfast, around 54,000 customers' new tariffs will be effective from 8 October and equates to an extra £12 per month.They said the increase was \"due to the increased cost of gas on the global energy markets caused by the prolonged conflict in the Middle East\".About 77,000 households and small businesses are in the Ten Towns area which includes Antrim, Armagh, Banbridge, Ballymena, Coleraine, Craigavon, Newry, Londonderry and more than 25 other towns and villages in the surrounding area,Announcing the tariff change, Sharleen Winning, head of regulation at Firmus Energy, said they \"cannot ignore the impact the situation in the Middle East is having on the cost of energy\".\"Unfortunately this has left us with no option other than to increase our tariffs.\"Winning said Firmus Energy have \"held off for as long as we could in the hope of a resolution that would lead to a reduction in the global wholesale prices\".\"We have demonstrated that when we are in a position to reduce tariffs, we do so without delay, and we hope to be able to do so again, once the global markets allow this.\"It comes after last week, Northern Ireland's largest provider of natural gas, SSE Airtricity, announced it is increasing prices by almost 19% from 1 October for its 200,000 customers.Leigh Greer from the the Utility Regulator said if customers are worried about paying for gas or electricity they should contact their supplier as soon as possible.\"Suppliers will work with customers to discuss payment options and the support available.\"We recognise that another increase in energy bills will be deeply concerning for households and small businesses, particularly for those already under pressure from the wider cost of living.\"Raymond Gormley, from the Consumer Council, said the increase hadn't come as a shock to him, but acknowledged it \"could be a really tough winter for consumers\".\"We consume about two-thirds of our energy in the winter months, so this is the time we're going to be using the energy and paying that bit more for it.\"Think about ways of saving money, think about the way you pay for your energy and the supplier you're with. Every little bit will help at this stage.\"",
      "source": "BBC",
      "category": "business",
      "tags": [],
      "author": null,
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-04T14:27:02.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://ichef.bbci.co.uk/ace/branded_news/1200/cpsprodpb/9fda/live/bb712b60-a842-11f1-b3a7-d5de760fef6e.jpg",
      "originalUrl": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqlw6g2x1lko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss",
      "readTimeMinutes": 2,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "hackernews-2026-09-04-burnt-out-on-a-three-day-week",
      "title": "Burnt out on a three-day week",
      "summary": "Article URL: https://jennywanger.com/articles/burnt-out-on-a-three-day-week/\nComments URL: https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49564960\nPoints: 54\n# Comments: 39",
      "body": "The exhaustion that rest can’t reach\nMaya spent over a decade at a healthtech company. She worked her way up from IC to people manager, running her own team, and she was thriving on the director track. Then she stepped back to deal with a health issue.\nNow she’s a fractional product leader three days a week for a startup while job-hunting to try and return to enterprise work. That health situation has since resolved. At three days a week, this is the lightest workload she’s had in many, many years. Yet she’s been struggling with feeling burnt out.\nThe most common advice I see for product leaders facing burnout is to rest. Take PTO. Meditate. Lighten the calendar. When rest doesn’t work, the next piece of advice is to quit and find a different company. In coaching, I’ve watched enough people follow both playbooks to know they don’t always fix the problem. Maya’s story can help show why.\nShe came to me for coaching after reading a post of mine sharing my experiences with how rest doesn’t always help. That resonated. She came to me to figure out what was going on under the surface.\nOn our call, she talked about the interview grind. She’d tried not doing too much prep, then tried a lot of rehearsal. No matter what she did, she’d go into the room and bomb it. The offers weren’t coming in.\nShe ran a hand over her face as she said: “That’s how I feel with burnout, that I’m doing so many things but nothing is ticking or landing.” She blamed the burnout for blocking her ability to be successful in interviews.\nWith all these job-hunt failures, a different question surfaced in her mind: was she actually not as good a product leader as she thought? She had recently switched to applying to lower-tier roles — less pay, less responsibility — because she thought that was the market signal. But she still wasn’t seeing success.\nShe knew she was burnt out. What she didn’t know was what kind, or whether other product leaders were going through it, or what she was supposed to do about it. Something beyond needing some more time off was going on here, so we dug in.\nWhen you listen to Maya describe her prior role, you can hear the joy in her voice. In her words, she could “get into the weeds yet really strategize.”\nLeaving a job she loves to deal with a health issue wasn’t an easy choice. And as things stabilized, she didn’t want her skills to go stale. This fractional role seemed like a perfect fit. Not too time-intensive, interesting enough. A startup, so new skills to be gained there.\nWhen talking with me, she admitted that she was on autopilot in her fractional role. “I’m holding back over there. I’d rather prioritize my energy on the job hunt.”\nShe added, almost surprised to hear herself, that this was the first time she’d actually said it out loud. The startup is on her resume. The startup is what she’s doing with her days. And by her own account, she’s giving less than her full effort.\nI took this moment to mirror her words back to her: “You have a belief about yourself that you are a high performing person. I heard it all through your story. And so here you are coasting through this startup, and that doesn’t align to how you see yourself. That’s the gap.”\nShe took a deep breath before she replied. “Exactly. There’s absolute truth to it because I know I’m not presenting the ideals or the work values that I have.”\nShe’s not just burned out from the misalignment at the startup. She’s also letting the bad interview rounds shape her self-perception. The gap between who she believes herself to be and the evidence she’s collecting about herself is widening from both sides at the same time.\nShe’s going into each interview feeling like a liar, presenting a version of herself she’s not currently living up to. That’s hard to sustain. Of course she’s exhausted.\nNaming it was the pivot for Maya. Once she could see the gap between how she saw herself and what she was actually bringing to work, she had somewhere to aim.\nSome burnout does come from doing too much. Rest fixes it, and if that’s what you have, take the rest. But over and over in coaching I’ve seen another version of this. Someone comes back from PTO, from a sabbatical, from cutting their hours, and the exhaustion follows them right back into the office. That’s the version I’ve been describing with Maya.\nWhat’s underneath isn’t workload. It’s a mismatch between who you are and what your current role or workplace is actually asking of you.\nSometimes the source is the environment: a role that doesn’t use what you’re best at, an org that keeps your real work off your calendar, a team dynamic that keeps you performing a version of yourself you don’t recognize.\nSometimes it’s you: how you’re engaging with the work, what you’re telling yourself about it, the parts of yourself you’ve been holding back.\nUsually it’s some of each. Either way, the mismatch shows up as a gap between the version of yourself you thought you’d be at work and the version that’s actually showing up.\nYou can be doing very little and be burnt out. You can be doing a lot and not be. The variable is the size of that mismatch.\nWhen you’re living out of alignment, you read everything as more evidence: the slow startup, the failed interview, the operating meeting that isn’t the work you wanted to be doing. The gap feeds itself. The longer it goes, the bigger the evidence pile against the version of yourself you want to be. That’s part of why it’s so hard to rest your way out of.\nMaya’s case shows the mechanism. She’s letting the interview pipeline define her view of herself. Every “no” becomes more proof that she’s not who she thought she was.\nAnd rest doesn’t touch this. Take the break, come back, and you land in the same patterns that made the gap in the first place.\nMaya’s case is one version. Let me introduce you to Jordan.\nJordan drowning in operating cadence\nJordan is a director who describes himself as a get-it-done person. A builder, mover, and someone you can rely on to deliver.\nWhen his company went through a reorg, the executive team put him in charge of leading the transformation of a critical product team. This was the work he was excited about — the chance to build something real and lead through change. Exactly the kind of assignment he’d taken this director role to do.\nThen challenges started piling on. His direct report went on parental leave so he started to cover for them. The executive team asked him to support comms for a different product. He still had some of his old responsibilities on his plate.\nHis day-to-day became focused on keeping the lights on — making sure meetings happened, holding operating rhythms together, keeping everyone informed about what was next. The transformation work he wanted to dig into kept getting sidelined by the operating cadence. At the end of every week he went home feeling like he had gotten nothing done.\nJordan told himself: “I’m not adding value.” What he really meant was “I’m not doing the work I came here to do.” His executive presentation went poorly because he couldn’t tell the story of team transformation he had committed to. He looked at a back-to-back calendar and saw all those meetings blocking him from success. And so that’s the story he told his CPO.\nHe felt frustrated and disappointed. And busy. So, so busy. So he didn’t really look too closely at those feelings, and instead put his head down to keep trying to stay above water. In coaching, he started mentioning that he felt burnout looming.\nWe started to recognize the internal inconsistency. Jordan had been narrating his work life as something happening to him: passive voice, victim grammar. His calendar keeping him from doing the work he wanted to do. His boss putting more responsibilities on his shoulders.\nHe wanted to be the person who’s resilient, who isn’t bitter, who doesn’t dwell on things. And right now he was the opposite of all three.\nHis burnout looked like overwork. It wasn’t. It was the gap between the leader he believed himself to be and the story he was telling about himself.\nThe gap they had in common\nFor both Maya and Jordan, what shifted in their heads wasn’t rest or a lighter schedule. It was the moment in coaching where we named what was actually going on — the gap between who they believed themselves to be and how they were showing up.\nFor Maya, the gap was between the high-performing strategic leader she believed herself to be and the version of her on autopilot at the startup, unable to land the interviews she thought should be within reach.\nFor Jordan, the gap was between the builder and get-it-done person he believed himself to be and the version of him buried under operating meetings, telling himself a story of failure.\nTheir workload looked very different, but a similar pattern haunted both of them.\nThe bigger the gap, the more exhausting it is to live in. And the more quickly the burnout becomes unsustainable.\nAs product leaders, busy calendars are part of the job. We can’t often take on a lighter workload or use time management to free up more time to meditate. And even if we could, that isn’t what fixes internal misalignment.\nFor both Maya and Jordan, the pivot wasn’t rest and it wasn’t quitting. It was naming the mismatch between who they were and what their situation was asking of them. Calling out the gap that had opened between how they saw themselves and how they were showing up.\nThat’s the first step, and it’s the hardest one to do on your own. In Part 2, I’ll walk through how we worked together to change that gap. And if you’re managing a team, how to help coach others through this type of challenge. Jordan and Maya’s stories continue there.\nIf you’re seeing yourself in Maya or Jordan, here are some questions I would ask:\nWhat version of yourself did you expect to be at work right now — and how does that compare to who’s actually showing up?\nWhere’s the mismatch coming from? The role, the workplace, how you’re navigating either, or some combination?\nImagine you were describing the gap to a friend. What would you say?\nSet a 10 minute timer and write your answers down. When you read them, what do you see?\nIf what you’re reading is landing in a heavier place than “this makes me want to reflect” — if you’re struggling with depression, anxiety, or exhaustion that’s affecting more than your work — please talk to a mental health professional. Coaching is a good fit for the “I recognize this pattern and want a thought partner to work through it” moment. It’s not a substitute for therapy or clinical support. Both matter, and they do different work.\nIf a thinking partner would help you work through the questions above, that’s what coaching is built for. Reach out.\nBecause of the sensitivity of this topic, I have changed names and key details of each person in this article.\nMany thanks to the pre-readers who helped me make sure this article treated this delicate topic with the care it deserves: Marcus Blankenship, Steven Hicks, George Sudarkoff.",
      "source": "Hacker News",
      "category": "tech",
      "tags": [],
      "author": "thelastgallon",
      "publishedAt": "2026-09-04T14:14:05.000Z",
      "imageUrl": "https://jennywanger.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/when-rest-isnt-fixing-your-burnout-header.png",
      "originalUrl": "https://jennywanger.com/articles/burnt-out-on-a-three-day-week/",
      "readTimeMinutes": 10,
      "language": "en"
    },
    {
      "id": "techcrunch-2026-09-04-less-than-24-hours-to-apply-for-your-techcrunch-disrupt-2026",
      "title": "Less than 24 hours to apply for your TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 Side Event",
      "summary": "Less than 24 hours left to apply to host a Side Event during TechCrunch Disrupt 2026 and make your mark in the Silicon Valley scene. Apply before the application closes tonight at midnight PT.",
      "body": "The clock is almost out. You have less than 24 hours left to apply to host a Side Event during TechCrunch Disrupt 2026. Applications close tonight at midnight PT.\nConnect with the entire Silicon Valley scene through October 10–16 by hosting your own Side Event. Bring your community, start an impactful conversation that’ll push the needle, and create an unforgettable experience.\nApply now to host a Side Event →\nCocktail Parties.Image Credits:Slava Blazer Photography\nPut the tech spotlight on your brand\nDisrupt brings thousands of founders, investors, partners, and tech leaders to San Francisco. Side Events give you another way to connect with that community — before, during, and after the conference.\nHost a meetup. Bring tech leaders together for a happy hour. Lead a panel or workshop. Create an experience your community will remember. The event is yours to plan.\nAn approved Side Event will:\nReach the Disrupt community and connect your audience with founders, investors, partners, and innovators from across the ecosystem.\nExtend your presence beyond the conference floor with an event during the broader “Disrupt week,” October 10–16.\nGet additional visibility from TechCrunch through placement on the official Side Events page, agenda, mobile app, attendee emails, a TechCrunch article, and social channels.\nGive your community an added reason to attend Disrupt with a 25% ticket discount to share with your network.\nApplying does not guarantee your event will be accepted or hosted as an official TechCrunch Disrupt Side Event.\nAll applications are reviewed by the TechCrunch events team. Your event must be approved before it can be included in the official Side Events program. All logistics and costs will be your responsibility.\nSo, if you want the opportunity to expand your brand reach, apply now.\nApplications close today at midnight PT. Don’t miss your chance to place your brand under the spotlight during one of the most anticipated tech conferences of the year, where 10,000+ tech leaders will converge in the Bay Area. Apply now →\nDon’t want to host a Side Event but still want to be a part of the global tech scene? Then grab your ticket with up to $200 off before rates increase on September 25.\nWhen you purchase through links in our articles, we may earn a small commission. This doesn’t affect our editorial independence.",
